window.__docsIndex=[{"u":"constitution.html","t":"Constitution — NativeWeb","s":"General","h":[{"i":"stack-constraints","t":"Stack constraints"},{"i":"architecture-invariants","t":"Architecture invariants"},{"i":"security--data","t":"Security / data"},{"i":"testing","t":"Testing"},{"i":"governance","t":"Governance"}],"b":"Constitution — NativeWeb Binding project rules. These are the WON'T-change decisions. Agents and humans obey them; changing one is a deliberate governance act, not a casual edit. This is NOT lessons-learned (see docs/lessons/) and NOT mechanical conventions (see docs/conventions.md). Status: draft · Last reviewed: Stack constraints (e.g. \"No React.\" / \"No Node.\" / \"NativeAOT — source-gen JSON only, no reflection.\") Architecture invariants (e.g. \"Every mutating flow is an HTML form action; app works with JS disabled.\") Security / data (e.g. \"AWS surface is SES+S3+SNS only.\" / rules for auth, input handling, data exposure) Testing (e.g. \"Core is zero-dependency; tests may use Testcontainers.\") Governance Supersedes ad-hoc practices. To change a rule: record the change + date + why, right here."},{"u":"conventions.html","t":"Conventions — NativeWeb","s":"General","h":[{"i":"file--directory-naming","t":"File & directory naming"},{"i":"git-workflow","t":"Git workflow"},{"i":"cleanup-policy","t":"Cleanup policy"},{"i":"docs-layout","t":"Docs layout"},{"i":"project-visibility","t":"Project visibility"},{"i":"readmes","t":"READMEs"},{"i":"instruction-file","t":"Instruction file"},{"i":"specs-freshness","t":"Specs freshness"},{"i":"response-compression-and-breach-spec-003-r32","t":"Response compression and BREACH (spec 003 R32)"},{"i":"caching-of-token-bearing-pages-spec-003-r30","t":"Caching of token-bearing pages (spec 003 R30)"},{"i":"options-validation-spec-003-r34","t":"Options validation (spec 003 R34)"},{"i":"progressive-enhancement-specs-004-005","t":"Progressive enhancement (specs 004, 005)"}],"b":"Conventions — NativeWeb Mechanical how-we-work rules. Defaults are inherited from ~/.claude/templates/project-rules.md; this file records project-specific deltas. Binding principles live in docs/constitution.md; project memory lives in docs/lessons/. File & directory naming kebab-case for files and dirs: mail-pipeline.md, data-model.sql. Except where an established convention dictates otherwise: README.md, CHANGELOG.md, LICENSE.md, Directory.Build.props, or language-mandated casing (C# PascalCase.cs). Unsure? Match the nearest existing sibling file. Git workflow main is release-only; dev is integration. Never commit directly to either. All work on named branches off dev (feat/ fix/ chore/ docs/ refactor/ test/). Commit often. Merge completed branches back to dev with --no-ff. Always merge once a branch is complete — never leave finished work unmerged. Release: dev -> main --no-ff + semver tag. No AI attribution. Ask before deleting branches. Cleanup policy NEVER auto-delete files, branches, or artifacts (the guards block it anyway). Instead: append every cleanup candidate to a running list, then generate a reviewed PowerShell script at scripts/cleanup/cleanup-<milestone>.ps1 for the user to run manually. Fixed interface, every script, both shells. Dry run is what a bare invocation does — there is no flag to request one. --execute / -e is the only way to act; not -Apply, -Force, --yes or -WhatIf. Anything irreversible beyond that (deleting untracked files, git rm, dropping tables, rewriting history) needs a second flag named for what it does, on top of --execute. A dry run always exits 0; --execute with a failed precondition exits non-zero. Script rules: $ErrorActionPreference='Stop' (or set -euo pipefail); safety gate (refuse if not on the expected branch); numbered sections; git branch -d (never -D); print the remaining state at the end; header comment naming the work and date that produced it. Docs layout Specs, plans, designs and architecture notes live under docs/, committed, never in a dot-directory and never untracked — they exist to be reviewed in a diff. docs/ specs/001-a-new-way-for-the-api/ # one dir per spec 001-spec.md 001-plan.md 001-tasks.md plans/2026-07-28-001-workflow-routing.md # plan-mode work with no spec architecture/001-storage-model.md Numbered always, zero-padded to three, next number allocated by scanning the directory. Dated as well where entries accumulate rather than being revised (plans/, decisions/); not in specs/, where a spec is updated in place. Date first, YYYY-MM-DD, so it sorts. An approved plan-mode plan is promoted out of its generated scratch name into docs/plans/ and committed with the branch's first commit. Project visibility work/ holds the files that answer \"what is going on here\" without reading the code. They are updated as part of the work, in the same branch, not batched to the end of a session. CHANGELOG.md # what shipped. At the ROOT, and written at release, not per commit. work/ tasklist.md # every task for the body of work underway. Stays put. milestones.md # where this is going. Coarse; changes rarely. todo.md # unscheduled backlog. Out-of-scope notes land here. status/ previous.md # the task that just finished current.md # the ONE task in flight next.md # the ONE task queued archive/ 2026-07-28-storage-move.md # a completed tasklist, one per shipped body of work tasklist.md is the task breakdown for the work currently underway. The status/ trio is a three-deep sliding window over it, one task each: current -> previous, next -> current, next unchecked tasklist entry -> next. The tasklist does not rotate; it is emptied and refilled when that body of work ships. Durable-tier work already has one at docs/specs/<NNN>-*/<NNN>-tasks.md — point at it rather than keeping a second copy. An item's path is todo.md -> tasklist.md -> status/next.md -> status/current.md -> status/previous.md -> CHANGELOG.md. When tasklist.md is fully ticked: summarise it into CHANGELOG.md under ## [Unreleased], move it to work/archive/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md, and start a fresh one. /status shows the rotation and flags staleness; /status roll advances it. docs/project/ and docs/status/ are the pre-2026-07-28 locations — read them if that is what the repo has, but do not move them without being asked, because the paths are referenced elsewhere. Check the layout after every task, not at the end of the body of work. A file left in the wrong place for six tasks is a file nobody will move. READMEs README.md at the repo root, always: what this is, how to run it, how to build it, and the layout table. Someone arriving cold should not have to read code to learn what the project is. A local README.md in any subdirectory whose purpose is not obvious from its name — a few lines saying what belongs there, what does not, and the naming rule if there is one. The test: if someone would have to open three files to work out what a directory is for, write one. Not every directory. src/ and tests/ explain themselves; work/archive/ and scripts/cleanup/ do not. Instruction file AGENTS.md is the instruction file. CLAUDE.md beside it holds the single line @AGENTS.md. Claude Code injects CLAUDE.md into context at load and does not inject AGENTS.md — but it does honour the @ import inside CLAUDE.md, which pulls the real file in with no tool call. Verified empirically with a canary in each file, both directions. Every other agent tool reads AGENTS.md directly. So there is one source of truth and a one-line shim nobody edits. Specs freshness When code diverges from a shipped spec, update docs/specs/NNN-*/spec.md (bump its Status: and add a last-verified: <date> line). /spec-status flags any spec older than its own plan/tasks as ⚠ stale. Response compression and BREACH (spec 003 R32) The app compresses text/html only, Brotli-first, with EnableForHttps=true (as do the products stamped from skeleton/). Compressing secret-bearing HTML over TLS is the precondition for BREACH-class attacks, so the acceptance is deliberate and bounded: Every secret that appears in rendered HTML is per-response, so the byte sequence an attacker would need to converge on changes every render and the compression-oracle loop BREACH depends on never stabilises. The inventory: the antiforgery request token (varies per render because Data Protection encrypts it with a randomized IV — there is no System.Web-style XOR mask, the IV is the whole mechanism), the CSP nonce (single-use by definition), the OAuth authorization code the consent page embeds in a hidden field (minted fresh on every GET of /oauth/authorize), and the TOTP secret on GET /auth/2fa/enroll (fresh per render). Invariant, not accident: the consent code and enroll secret staying freshly minted per render is load-bearing for this acceptance. An optimization that reuses the pending authorization (or the staged TOTP secret) across refreshes would put a stable secret in the same compressed response as attacker-chosen state — exactly the oracle this section claims cannot exist. Such a change must revisit this section, not just its own endpoint. HTML-encoding user input is XSS hygiene, not a BREACH mitigation — token alphabets (base64/base32/hex) pass through HtmlEncoder untouched, so encoded reflection compresses against a secret exactly as raw reflection would. The reflection leg of BREACH is instead starved by the per-IP rate limits (tens per minute, where the oracle needs thousands of probes) and defused by the previous bullet (no stable secret to converge on). OAuth token/introspection responses are JSON and not on the mime list; they are never compressed. If a future page acquires a secret that survives across renders, that page — not the mime list — is the thing to fix. Caching of token-bearing pages (spec 003 R30) No shared-cache headers and no ETags on session-bound HTML: SecurityHeadersMiddleware stamps Cache-Control: no-store (+ Pragma: no-cache where RFC 6749 demands) on every sensitive content type and redirect. Fingerprinted static assets keep their long-lived immutable caching — the split is by content type, never blanket. Static pages (R38) get no-cache + ETag revalidation instead: they carry no tokens, so revalidation is safe and cheap. Options validation (spec 003 R34) The apps deliberately have no IOptions<T> classes to ValidateOnStart: configuration is read imperatively in Program.cs with fail-fast guards that throw before Build() on malformed values (see StartupConfigGuardTests, which boot the real binary against broken config and assert the refusal). A future options class must either keep that shape or register with .ValidateOnStart() — a config error surfacing as a first-request hang is the AOT lesson this rule closes. Progressive enhancement (specs 004, 005) Browser-facing markup uses the data-nw-* enhancement layer, normalized to hx-* by the vendored boot.js (source libs/nativeweb.web/wwwroot/_nativeweb/boot.js, delivered by NativeWeb.Web.targets, loaded defer before htmx). State-changing endpoints are POST-only verb routes; transport is derived from the element's own method/action/href, and an authored hx-* attribute always wins. On top of that sit the spec 005 UX defaults: boot.js busy states and focus-after-swap, cookie flash messages, access keys, native-constraint mirroring with field-level server errors, /version, and the any-content-type rule. Full conventions: progressive-enhancement.md."},{"u":"00-quick-start-readme.html","t":"NativeWeb","s":"Quick start","h":[{"i":"then-as-you-need-it","t":"Then, as you need it"}],"b":"NativeWeb A server-first architecture for ASP.NET Core: Minimal APIs, server-rendered HTML, NativeAOT, and no client framework. Explicit code over convention, compile-time errors over runtime blanks, and a page that works before any JavaScript runs. New here, read these two in order. Everything else on this site answers a specific question and is not written to be read straight through. Read What you get Getting started A clean clone to a running application and a green suite, then stamping your own product from skeleton/. Tutorial: your first feature One feature through every layer it touches — a read model, a JauntyQ query, a renderer, an endpoint, and a test at each of the three tiers. Then, as you need it Architecture — why the stack is what it is, and what it refuses to do. Everything else assumes it. Progressive enhancement — the data-nw-* conventions. Mandatory for every browser-facing page. Configuration — every setting, its default, and what a wrong or absent value actually costs. Template diagnostics — NWT001–NWT016, what each build error means and how to fix it. Security baseline — the ASVS-derived checklist NativeWeb and the products stamped from it are held to. Migration lock review — how long db/migrate.sh holds its locks. Read before pointing it at a live database. Specs, plans, decisions and lessons live in the repository rather than on this site; they record how the project is built rather than how to use it."},{"u":"00-quick-start-adopting-in-an-existing-app.html","t":"Adopting NativeWeb in an existing application","s":"Quick start","h":[{"i":"1-reference-the-framework","t":"1. Reference the framework"},{"i":"why-not-packages","t":"Why not packages"},{"i":"the-project-references","t":"The project references"},{"i":"declare-your-templates","t":"Declare your templates"},{"i":"2-wire-the-pipeline","t":"2. Wire the pipeline"},{"i":"two-things-that-stop-the-process-if-you-skip-them","t":"Two things that stop the process if you skip them"},{"i":"3-delete-what-the-framework-now-owns","t":"3. Delete what the framework now owns"},{"i":"4-what-you-must-keep","t":"4. What you must keep"},{"i":"5-constraints-you-inherit","t":"5. Constraints you inherit"},{"i":"6-check-it-worked","t":"6. Check it worked"},{"i":"if-you-get-stuck","t":"If you get stuck"}],"b":"Adopting NativeWeb in an existing application getting-started.md is the greenfield path: clone, build, run tools/new-product.ps1, get a stamped product. This is the other one. You have an ASP.NET Core application already, with its own solution, its own Directory.Build.props and its own deployment, and you want the framework underneath it. Read this if you have written a page shell, a CSP nonce, a clean-URL rewrite, flash messages or content-hashed asset URLs by hand. All five ship in libs/nativeweb.web. 1. Reference the framework Checkout mode is the supported way to consume NativeWeb. Clone this repository as a sibling of your product: your-org/ nativeweb/ <- this repository your-product/ <- yours No jauntyq/ sibling is needed. NativeWeb resolves JauntyQ from the pinned Extrode.JauntyQ.* packages on nuget.org, which restore without a credential. Build with -p:JauntyQUsePackages=false only if you want a sibling jauntyq checkout instead. The license terms still apply to what you ship (see the constraints at the end of this page). Then add to your product's Directory.Build.props. Both casings are probed: the repository is nativeweb on disk, and getting-started.md tells people to clone it as NativeWeb. <PropertyGroup> <NativeWebRoot Condition=\"'$(NativeWebRoot)' == '' and Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\\nativeweb')\">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\\nativeweb<\/NativeWebRoot> <NativeWebRoot Condition=\"'$(NativeWebRoot)' == '' and Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\\NativeWeb')\">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\\NativeWeb<\/NativeWebRoot> <NativeWebRoot Condition=\"'$(NativeWebRoot)' == ''\">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\\nativeweb<\/NativeWebRoot> <\/PropertyGroup> <Target Name=\"EnsureNativeWebRoot\" BeforeTargets=\"BeforeBuild\" Condition=\"!Exists('$(NativeWebRoot)\\libs\\nativeweb.render\\nativeweb.render.csproj')\"> <Error Text=\"NativeWeb checkout not found at '$(NativeWebRoot)'. Pass -p:NativeWebRoot=&lt;path&gt;.\" /> <\/Target> <Target Name=\"ReportFrameworkResolution\" BeforeTargets=\"BeforeBuild\"> <Message Importance=\"high\" Text=\"NativeWeb: checkout mode ($(NativeWebRoot))\" /> <\/Target> The guard matters. An unresolved root drops the project references with nothing worse than warning MSB9008, so the build carries on and fails later as a pile of CS0246s pointing at your own code. Without the guard, the error you read is never the error you have. skeleton/Directory.Build.props carries the full version of this block, including package mode, and is the file to copy from. Probe relatively, and name no parent directory. A hard-coded ancestor breaks every build the day that directory is renamed. If your product builds from a git worktree, add the deeper probe too — a worktree sits three directories down, so a single sibling probe resolves to nothing and every restore in every worktree fails: <NativeWebRoot Condition=\"'$(NativeWebRoot)' == '' and Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\\..\\..\\nativeweb')\">$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\\..\\..\\nativeweb<\/NativeWebRoot> Why not packages The thirteen packable projects under libs/ carry IsPackable and a PackageId, and skeleton/Directory.Build.props already has a -p:NativeWebUsePackages=true mode. Do not use it yet. The packages publish to nuget.org as Extrode.NativeWeb.* from a v* tag (../02-reference/versioning.md), but none has been released, so package mode has never restored end to end. There is also no stated semver guarantee, support window or deprecation path for a consumed library yet. See ../decisions/2026-08-30-004-package-mode-for-consumers.md for what it obliges. Until that lands, a checkout is the supported model and you upgrade by pulling. The project references Add the ones you need. nativeweb.render and its generator are the minimum for rendering: <ItemGroup> <ProjectReference Include=\"$(NativeWebRoot)\\libs\\nativeweb.render\\nativeweb.render.csproj\" /> <ProjectReference Include=\"$(NativeWebRoot)\\libs\\nativeweb.render.generator\\nativeweb.render.generator.csproj\" OutputItemType=\"Analyzer\" ReferenceOutputAssembly=\"false\" /> <ProjectReference Include=\"$(NativeWebRoot)\\libs\\nativeweb.web\\nativeweb.web.csproj\" /> <\/ItemGroup> A generator is referenced as an analyzer, with ReferenceOutputAssembly=\"false\". Reference it as an ordinary project and it compiles into your output and generates nothing. Import the assets targets by hand. A PackageReference would pick this up on its own; a ProjectReference does not, so nothing contributes the framework's browser assets (boot.js and the design-system stylesheets under /_nativeweb/) or its shared templates to your product, and every asset 404s at run time with no build error: <Import Project=\"$(NativeWebRoot)\\libs\\nativeweb.web\\build\\NativeWeb.Web.targets\" /> Declare your templates The render generator never reads disk — it sees only what the compiler hands it, so your templates and locale catalogs have to be AdditionalFiles. Nothing warns when this is missing: the generator emits no classes and your first reference to one is a CS0103. <ItemGroup> <AdditionalFiles Include=\"templates\\**\\*.html\" /> <AdditionalFiles Include=\"locales\\*.json\" /> <\/ItemGroup> locales/en.json is the root catalog and every {{t:...}} key must exist in it, so a product that ships one language still ships that file. 2. Wire the pipeline Two calls, in your composition root: WebApplicationBuilder builder = WebApplication.CreateSlimBuilder(args); NativeWebOptions options = NativeWebOptions.FromConfiguration(builder.Configuration, \"MyProduct\"); builder.AddNativeWebWeb(options, typeof(Program).Assembly); WebApplication app = builder.Build(); app.UseNativeWebPipeline(Locales.All); app.MapNativeWebEndpoints(options, typeof(Program).Assembly); // your own routes here app.Run(); The two calls take different things and the difference is not a slip. UseNativeWebPipeline takes the locale ids you ship — Locales.All, generated from your own locales/*.json — plus an optional Action<IApplicationBuilder> for authentication, which must be registered there rather than afterwards or every authenticated form post is a 400. MapNativeWebEndpoints is the one that takes the options, because it reads the security contact and the readiness window from them. \"MyProduct\" is both the application name and the configuration section, so every setting reads from MyProduct:. Pass a third argument to FromConfiguration when they differ. Every key is in ../02-reference/configuration.md. Pass typeof(Program).Assembly rather than letting it default to the entry assembly: under a WebApplicationFactory test the entry assembly is the test assembly, not yours, so /version reports its version instead. AddNativeWebWeb is a series of registrations behind one call, and every one of them is a public method on NativeWebPipeline. If you need to sit between two of them, call them yourself. Two things that stop the process if you skip them wwwroot/pages/error.html is required. The error shell is loaded once at startup, from a static file, so that an error page never depends on the renderer or the database — either of which may be the thing that just failed. It is your file, not the framework's: copy apps/sample/wwwroot/pages/error.html and restyle it, keeping the {{title}} and {{message}} placeholders, which are literal-replaced. Without it the host prints wwwroot/pages/error.html is missing and exits 78 — a refused configuration, not a crash. Generated code lands in your RootNamespace. A template at templates/hello.html in a project rooted at your_product becomes your_product.Templates.Hello. Declare your own code in that namespace, as apps/sample does. A using your_product; in a file outside it is not enough: a using directive imports a namespace's types and not its nested namespaces, so Templates.Hello still fails to resolve while Locales beside it resolves fine. 3. Delete what the framework now owns If you have Delete it and use A layout shell gathering nonce, antiforgery token and locale Chrome.For(context, assets, antiforgery, locale) CSP with a per-request nonce, HSTS, permissions policy SecurityHeadersMiddleware, wired by UseNativeWebPipeline A clean-URL rewrite and its startup route map StaticPagesMiddleware A one-shot flash cookie Flash.Set / Flash.Take Content-hashed asset URLs AssetUrls A hand-rolled /healthz, /readyz, /version MapNativeWebEndpoints Passphrase hashing, opaque tokens, PII masking libs/nativeweb.security A hand-written OAuth authorization-code flow libs/nativeweb.signin Delete one at a time and run your tests between. The framework's versions differ in behaviour at the edges — Chrome.Anonymous deliberately empties every per-request field so a public page can be output-cached, which a hand-rolled shell usually does not do. 4. What you must keep Your own routes, data access and domain. NativeWeb has no ORM. Data access is JauntyQ, source-generated over PostgreSQL, and it is a separate adoption decision from this one. Your wwwroot. Framework assets arrive through an MSBuild .targets that contributes them to your static web assets; you do not copy them. Third-party files (HTMX, Alpine.js, fonts) are yours to vendor, as apps/sample/wwwroot/vendor/ does. NativeAOT discipline, if you publish AOT. The framework builds with TreatWarningsAsErrors and IsAotCompatible. A reference of yours that is not trim-compatible fails your publish, not ours. 5. Constraints you inherit PostgreSQL only. No provider abstraction exists to swap. Minimal APIs only. No MVC, no Controllers, no Razor Pages, no Blazor. HTMX and Alpine are vendored, never from a CDN. JauntyQ's source and packages are under ISL-R 1.2. Code its generator emits is yours under its Output Exception, and Extrode.JauntyQ.Runtime may ship inside your built output under its Redistribution Exception. Nothing else of JauntyQ's may be redistributed. 6. Check it worked dotnet build The ReportFrameworkResolution target above prints NativeWeb: checkout mode (<path>), so a wrong root is visible in the log rather than at the first CS0246. Then run your app: Request Expected /healthz 200 once the pipeline is mounted /readyz 503 until a connection string is configured — the answer that gates load-balancer traffic, and the right one before you have one /version 200, reporting the assembly you passed to MapNativeWebEndpoints your own template route 200, with the CSP, X-Content-Type-Options, X-Frame-Options, Referrer-Policy and Permissions-Policy headers already set Those four are what this guide was checked against: a stock dotnet new web project taken through every step above, serving a generated template page. Before you deploy behind a proxy, set {Section}:ForwardedHeaders:KnownProxies. This is the one step whose omission the table above cannot see: without it the app builds, boots, serves every page and is wrong about who is calling. RemoteIpAddress is the proxy's address rather than the client's, so every request lands in one rate-limit partition and every access log names the same host. Nothing fails and nothing warns. Set it to the comma-separated addresses of the hops in front of you, and confirm by logging HttpContext.Connection.RemoteIpAddress on one request from outside. This repository's own deploy.sh refuses to promote a release without it; your deployment has no such guard unless you write one. To assert them rather than eyeball them, reference Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing from a test project and drive the four through a WebApplicationFactory<Program>. Top-level statements make Program internal, so add public partial class Program; to the end of the file or the factory will not close over it — and this is where passing typeof(Program).Assembly earns its keep, because the entry assembly under a factory is the test assembly, not the product's. If you get stuck ../../libs/nativeweb.web/README.md — the pipeline in detail, including what each registration does. ../02-reference/configuration.md — every setting and its failure mode. Three of them stop the process at startup on purpose. ../02-reference/breaking-changes.md — what changed between the version you cloned and the one you pulled."},{"u":"00-quick-start-getting-started.html","t":"Getting started","s":"Quick start","h":[{"i":"before-you-start","t":"Before you start"},{"i":"1-clone","t":"1. Clone"},{"i":"2-build","t":"2. Build"},{"i":"3-run-it-with-no-database","t":"3. Run it with no database"},{"i":"4-run-the-tests","t":"4. Run the tests"},{"i":"next","t":"Next"}],"b":"Getting started From a clean clone to a running application and a green suite, then to a product of your own. NativeWeb is a framework. You run the reference app in apps/sample to see it work, then stamp a new product out of skeleton/ and work in that, not here. Before you start The requirements are in the README. PostgreSQL is optional for everything in steps 1 to 3. 1. Clone git clone <this repository> NativeWeb cd NativeWeb Anywhere you like. JauntyQ restores from packages, so nothing here looks for a sibling checkout unless you build with -p:JauntyQUsePackages=false. The directory can be called NativeWeb or nativeweb. A stamped product probes for both. It matters only on a case-sensitive filesystem with a third name: then set NativeWebRoot instead of relying on the probe. 2. Build dotnet build JauntyQ resolves from the pinned Extrode.JauntyQ.* packages on nuget.org. No credential is needed. 3. Run it with no database dotnet run --project apps/sample Open http://localhost:5399/. The home page shows the newest published articles, rendered through compiled templates. Each title links to /articles/{slug} and each tag to /tags/{slug}. /tags lists every tag with its count. /articles is the same listing with a filter. The filter posts over htmx and swaps only the list. The route also answers as JSON and CSV. /admin/articles is the scaffolded CRUD behind the demo sign-in. A blank slug is derived from the title. To edit and see the change without a manual restart: dotnet watch run --project apps/sample --no-hot-reload Both parts matter. Without --no-hot-reload the watcher logs nothing on a change and the app keeps serving the old markup. Templates are AdditionalFiles, which dotnet watch does not track by default, so nativeweb.sample.csproj adds <Watch Include=\"templates\\**\\*.html\" /> (and the same for locales\\*.json). A template edit re-runs the source generator, so the loop is a rebuild and a restart, a few seconds. A missing connection string is not an error. It selects an in-memory store, so the demo and the test tiers work with no database. It is a development convenience, not a supported deployment mode. Configure ConnectionStrings:Sample and the same code paths run against PostgreSQL through JauntyQ. apps/sample/Program.cs explains why the choice is made in the application and not in a library. apps/sample runs over plain HTTP, so there is no HTTPS step. 4. Run the tests Every feature has up to three tiers, and the project requires all of them. See AGENTS.md. Tier What it proves Needs Unit (xUnit) one unit against its own inputs, failure paths included nothing Integration (WebApplicationFactory) the real pipeline, routing and middleware PostgreSQL, for the projects that use it End-to-end (Playwright) a real browser against a real server, including the no-JavaScript path Chromium, installed once Install the browser once, after building the e2e project: pwsh tests/nativeweb.scaffold.tests.e2e/bin/Debug/net10.0/playwright.ps1 install chromium The integration and e2e projects that touch the sample database need nativeweb_sample_test. db/sample/README.md has the commands that create it. Run one project at a time. A project that stops being discovered then fails by name instead of vanishing from a glob. Every project is named nativeweb.<lib>.tests.<tier>: # unit tier dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.repo.tests.unit dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.render.tests.unit dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.web.tests.unit dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.forms.tests.unit dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.security.tests.unit dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.mail.tests.unit dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.scaffold.tests.unit dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.permissions.tests.unit dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.signin.tests.unit dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.sample.tests.unit # integration tier dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.forms.tests.integration dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.scaffold.tests.integration dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.permissions.tests.integration dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.signin.tests.integration dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.sample.tests.integration # end-to-end tier dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.forms.tests.e2e dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.scaffold.tests.e2e dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.permissions.tests.e2e dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.signin.tests.e2e # NativeAOT tier dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.signin.tests.aot Do not run several e2e projects back to back in one shell: they can contend. If a whole e2e tier fails, re-run it alone before believing it. Build your own product skeleton/ is a tokenized product shell: app pipeline, templates, static pages, migration runner, three test tiers, CI, deploy units and the repo operating files, with __PRODUCT__ and __product__ where your name goes. tools/new-product.ps1 stamps a copy. 1. Stamp it. A bare invocation is a dry run; -Execute acts. pwsh tools/new-product.ps1 -Name Probe -Slug probe -Destination ../probe pwsh tools/new-product.ps1 -Name Probe -Slug probe -Destination ../probe -Execute The dry run lists every file it would copy, stamp and rename. The real run also does git init, an initial commit and a dev branch, so you get a repository and not just a directory. Two things the stamp needs that nothing else tells you. A global protected-branch git hook refuses the first commit on the product's main, which fails the stamp with every file already written: prefix the command with GIT_ALLOW_PROTECTED=1 if you have such a hook. A runner with no git identity fails the same step, which is why CI sets one. To check that skeleton/ still builds before you stamp the product you mean to keep: deno run -A tools/stamp-check.ts It stamps into tmp/, builds, and runs the stamped unit tier. Add --with-database to run the integration tier too, once its role and database exist. Put the product beside your NativeWeb checkout, as ../probe does above. The stamped product resolves the framework libraries from that sibling by default. 2. Create the role and database. Substitute your slug for probe throughout. CREATE ROLE probe_app LOGIN PASSWORD '...'; CREATE DATABASE probe_dev OWNER probe_app; 3. Apply the schema. migrate.sh reads its settings from the environment and defaults to the local dev names, so this usually needs only a password: cd ../probe PGPASSWORD=... bash db/migrate.sh The first migration runs as postgres because it creates the citext extension, which the app role may not. Every later one runs as the app role. Re-runs skip what is already applied. 4. Build and test. export Probe__Db__ConnectionString=\"Host=127.0.0.1;Database=probe_dev;Username=probe_app;Password=...\" dotnet build dotnet test --project tests/probe.tests.unit dotnet test --project tests/probe.tests.integration dotnet test --project tests/probe.tests.e2e The integration tier needs <Product>__Db__ConnectionString in the environment. Without it the tier runs, but /readyz reports ServiceUnavailable and one fact fails. The double underscore is the environment form of Probe:Db:ConnectionString. If the product is not beside NativeWeb, set the root as an environment variable, not a -p: flag. -p:NativeWebRoot=... works for dotnet build and then fails the e2e tier, because that tier's fixture runs its own dotnet publish and command-line properties do not cross into a child process. Environment variables do: export NativeWebRoot=/path/to/NativeWeb The symptom is a publish failure inside the e2e fixture reading \"Skipping project … because it was not found\", which names NativeWeb's libraries and does not look like a path problem. 5. Work in your product, not here. Its own README.md and AGENTS.md are yours to fill in. Come back to this repository only to pick up framework changes. Then read, in this order: the architecture for why the stack is shaped as it is, the security baseline for the posture your product inherits, and progressive enhancement before touching a template. The full index is docs/README.md. Next tutorial-first-feature.md walks one feature through every layer: endpoint, template, query, and a test at each of the three tiers. To bring NativeWeb into an app you already have, see adopting-in-an-existing-app.md."},{"u":"00-quick-start-tutorial-first-feature.html","t":"Tutorial — build your first feature","s":"Quick start","h":[{"i":"why-this-feature-is-hand-written-at-all","t":"Why this feature is hand-written at all"},{"i":"1-the-read-model-and-its-seam","t":"1. The read model and its seam"},{"i":"2-the-query","t":"2. The query"},{"i":"3-the-template","t":"3. The template"},{"i":"4-the-endpoint","t":"4. The endpoint"},{"i":"5-the-wiring","t":"5. The wiring"},{"i":"6-the-unit-tier","t":"6. The unit tier"},{"i":"7-the-integration-tier","t":"7. The integration tier"},{"i":"8-the-end-to-end-tier","t":"8. The end-to-end tier"},{"i":"9-perturb-every-green-test","t":"9. Perturb every green test"},{"i":"run-it-all","t":"Run it all"},{"i":"what-to-take-to-your-own-feature","t":"What to take to your own feature"}],"b":"Tutorial — build your first feature One feature, through every layer it touches: a read model, a JauntyQ query, a template, an endpoint, and a test at each of the three tiers. Nothing is deferred to a later page, because the layers only make sense against each other. The finished feature is already in this repository. It is GET /articles in apps/sample — a public, read-only page listing published articles, newest first, with a filter. Run it now, before reading further: dotnet run --project apps/sample then open http://localhost:5399/articles. You are looking at the result of every step below. The home page at /, the article page at /articles/{slug} and the tag pages under /tags are the same pattern applied again: a reader interface, a template, a route. Read apps/sample/PublicSite.cs once this walk-through makes sense. Follow along in your own product, not here. If you have a stamped product from the getting-started guide, build the equivalent page against its own table and read this repository's files as the worked answer. Or just read: the layering is the same, and apps/sample is the smaller place to see it whole. The files, in the order this page covers them: File Layer apps/sample/PublishedArticles.cs the read model and its seam, plus both implementations db/sample/queries/Articles/ListPublished.sql the query apps/sample/templates/articles/index.html the page template apps/sample/templates/articles/results.html the list fragment the page and the filter share apps/sample/PublicArticleEndpoints.cs the endpoint apps/sample/Program.cs the wiring tests/nativeweb.sample.tests.unit/PublicArticlesTests.cs unit tier tests/nativeweb.scaffold.tests.integration/PublicFeedTests.cs integration tier tests/nativeweb.scaffold.tests.e2e/PublicFeedTests.cs e2e tier Why this feature is hand-written at all apps/sample already has a complete admin UI over the same table, and not one line of it exists in source: app.MapScaffolding() generates every route from the attributes on the Article model. So the first question a tutorial owes you is why this page is not generated too. Because scaffolding earns its place on CRUD over a table, and nothing else. The admin screens are forms over columns, which is exactly the shape an attribute can describe. A public feed is a product decision — which rows, in what order, showing which fields, at what page size — and none of that is a property of the table. A real product is mostly the hand-written kind. The scaffolding is there so the CRUD costs you nothing, not so the product does. What scaffolding can and cannot express is in libs/nativeweb.scaffold/README.md. 1. The read model and its seam apps/sample/PublishedArticles.cs opens with a record and an interface: public readonly record struct PublishedArticle( string Id, string Slug, string Title, string? Author, DateTime CreatedAt, IReadOnlyList<TagRef> Tags, DateOnly? PublishedAt = null); public interface IPublishedArticles { ValueTask<IReadOnlyList<PublishedArticle>> RecentAsync(int take, CancellationToken ct); // ...plus BySlugAsync, CountViewAsync, ByTagAsync and SitemapAsync for the other public pages } Seven fields, where the Article model has eleven: no Body, no Status, no CategoryKey, no view count. That narrowing is the point, not an optimisation. Article is the scaffolding's model: its shape is decided by what the admin form edits, so handing it to a public page means the page silently gains a field the day someone adds one to the form. A read model that names exactly what it renders cannot drift that way — and Body never reaching a list page is a decision you can see rather than one you have to trust. The interface is a seam for the same reason IScaffoldStore<T> is one: Program.cs decides which implementation answers, and nothing above it learns whether a database exists. Two implementations sit below it in the same file. In memory, filtering and sorting are the caller's job, because the store seam offers no way to express either: return all .Where(a => a.Status == ArticleStatus.Published) .OrderByDescending(a => a.PublishedAt ?? DateOnly.FromDateTime(a.CreatedAt)) .ThenByDescending(a => a.CreatedAt) .ThenByDescending(a => a.Id, StringComparer.Ordinal) .ToList(); Status is an enum with three members and only Published is public: a Draft and an Archived row are equally absent from every public page, and the article page answers 404 for both. The order is the publication date the admin set, a row without one dated by its creation day, so an article can be published today and dated last week. The category the admin picked is not in the read model at all, which is the same narrowing again: the public site does not show it yet, so it does not carry it. Over PostgreSQL, the same class does nothing but map rows, because the filter and the ordering moved into SQL: List<Row> rows = await db.Articles.ListPublishedAsync(take, ct).ConfigureAwait(false); Both are registered as singletons. Neither holds per-request state, and the JauntyQ one takes a connection from the pool per call — the reasoning is in JauntyArticleStore's remarks, next to it. Keeping both is deliberate. The in-memory path is why the demo, the integration tier and the browser tier all work with no database at all, and the pair is the clearest statement this codebase makes that persistence is a registration rather than a rewrite. 2. The query All data access goes through JauntyQ — no EF Core, no Dapper, no hand-written ADO.NET — and a JauntyQ query is a .sql file that the source generator turns into a typed method. There is no step where you write the C# for it. Drop the file in db/sample/queries/Articles/, build, and db.Articles.ListPublishedAsync exists. The generator, the schema snapshot it reads, and why apps/sample hosts it directly rather than through a data library of its own, are in db/sample/README.md and db/README.md. Read the first one now if you have not: it also has the two CREATE DATABASE lines this feature's tests need. -- @params take:int SELECT id, title, author, body, view_count, created_at, slug, status, published_at, category_key FROM articles WHERE status = 'published' ORDER BY COALESCE(published_at, (created_at AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')::date) DESC, created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT @take; The committed file also carries a long comment; the query itself is the lines above. Two things in it are worth the walk. -- @params take:int. The generator infers a parameter's type by looking at the typed column it is compared against. @take appears in LIMIT, where there is no column, so it infers nothing and says so — JNT4003 — rather than guessing. Declaring it is the fix. The ORDER BY has to match an index. A query that sorts on columns no index covers sorts at runtime, and JauntyQ raises JNT8007 about it. JauntyQ rates that a Warning; TreatWarningsAsErrors is on repo-wide (AGENTS.md), so here it stops the build. This query is covered by the partial expression index articles_public_order_idx in db/sample/migrations/0004_status_dates_categories.sql, which has the same three descending keys over status = 'published'. A btree can be walked backwards but not half-backwards, so the directions in the query must match the index's own. When a table is small and will stay small, the other honest answer is to accept the sort with -- @allow-sort <reason>. The reason is mandatory: a bare directive is rejected with JNT3008. The exemption also expires on its own. Once an index covers the sort, JNT8012 fires on the directive and says it suppresses nothing, so an exemption cannot outlive what justified it. The JNT diagnostics are JauntyQ's own and are documented upstream, not in this repository. The NWT codes in template-diagnostics.md are a different family belonging to the template engine, and do not apply here. Every column, though the page shows a few. A whole-table projection makes the generated row type Extrode.JauntyQ.Generated.Article — the same type List.sql already produces — so this query adds a method and not a second row shape to learn. Narrow it when row width costs something; on a demo table it buys nothing and costs a type. The order ends in created_at DESC, id DESC. The leading key is the publication date, with a row that has none dated by its creation day. created_at defaults to NOW(), so a scripted or seeded insert can give several rows one timestamp. Without the id tiebreak the page order is whatever the database happens to return: stable enough to look fine locally, unstable enough to reorder between requests in production. 3. The template A page is a compiled template. apps/sample/templates/articles/index.html declares its inputs in a header comment, and the render generator turns the file into a typed method at build time: <!--#template @layout layout/page @param string Query @param int Take @param string AntiforgeryFieldName @param string AntiforgeryToken @param System.Collections.Generic.IReadOnlyList<NativeWeb.Sample.PublishedArticle> Articles --> <h1 class=\"page__title\">{{t:articles.title}}<\/h1> <p class=\"page__lede\">{{t:articles.lede}}<\/p> Templates are AdditionalFiles in nativeweb.sample.csproj; there is no view engine and no runtime parsing. Call the generated Templates.Articles.Index with the wrong parameters and the build fails, so the compiler tells you a field is missing. Build errors from the template itself are listed in template-diagnostics.md. Why the architecture makes this trade — server-rendered HTML, no SPA build pipeline, everything compiled into one NativeAOT binary — is native-web-architecture.md. The list itself is a second template, articles/results.html, which the page includes with {{> articles/results Articles=Articles}}. It is split out because the filter swaps it alone. Interpolations are encoded by default. <h2 class=\"articles__title\"><a href=\"/articles/{{article.Slug}}\">{{article.Title}}<\/a><\/h2> The title and the author come out of a database that an admin form writes to, which makes them attacker-influenced input on a public page. {{article.Title}} is HTML-encoded without you asking. A unit test asserts that this default is real (Results_EncodesTitleAndAuthor). The standard this is held to is security-baseline.md. The empty case says something. {{if Articles.Count == 0}} <p class=\"articles__empty\">{{t:articles.empty}}<\/p> {{else}} An empty feed is a normal state — a fresh database has no published rows — and an empty <ul> reads as a broken page. The nullable author gets the same treatment one level down: {{if article.Author != null}} omits the element entirely, so an author-less article has no stray punctuation. Text comes from the locale catalog. {{t:articles.title}} reads apps/sample/locales/en.json. Every key a template uses must exist in the root catalog. The page works without JavaScript. The filter form carries a real method=\"post\" and action=\"/articles\" as well as the htmx attributes: <form class=\"articles__filter\" method=\"post\" action=\"/articles\" hx-post=\"/articles\" hx-target=\"#results\" hx-select=\"#results\" hx-swap=\"outerHTML\"> With JavaScript the response swaps only #results; without it the form posts and the whole page comes back. progressive-enhancement.md is mandatory for any page that gains an interaction. 4. The endpoint apps/sample/PublicArticleEndpoints.cs maps the route with MapNegotiated, which serves one path in several representations: endpoints.MapNegotiated(\"/articles\", [Format.Html, Format.Json, Format.Csv], async (http, format) => { IPublishedArticles articles = http.RequestServices.GetRequiredService<IPublishedArticles>(); IReadOnlyList<PublishedArticle> recent = await articles .RecentAsync(PageSize, http.RequestAborted).ConfigureAwait(false); if (format == Format.Json) { return FormatResults.Json(recent, SampleJson.Default.IReadOnlyListPublishedArticle); } if (format == Format.Csv) { return FormatResults.Csv(CsvHeaders, recent.Select(CsvRow)); } IAntiforgery antiforgery = http.RequestServices.GetRequiredService<IAntiforgery>(); AntiforgeryTokenSet tokens = antiforgery.GetAndStoreTokens(http); return Page(http, recent, query: null, take: PageSize, tokens); }) .RequireRateLimiting(NativeWebPipeline.GeneralRateLimitPolicy); /articles answers the page, /articles.json and /articles.csv (or an Accept header naming either type) answer the same rows as data, and any other extension is a 404 that never reaches the store. The mechanism is in the \"Negotiated routes\" section of libs/nativeweb.web/README.md. Page renders through the compiled layout: return Templates.Articles.Index.Result( locale: locale, Title: locale[\"title.articles\"], AntiforgeryToken: tokens.RequestToken ?? \"\", FlashOk: flash.Ok, FlashError: flash.Error, Query: query ?? \"\", Take: take, AntiforgeryFieldName: tokens.FormFieldName, Articles: articles); The filter posts to the same path through MapFormPost<ArticleFilter>(\"/articles\", ...). The handler takes the bound ArticleFilter and returns the results fragment alone for an htmx request (HX-Request header) or the whole page for a plain form post. Same handler, same data, one branch. Minimal APIs only — no MVC, no controllers, no Razor Pages. The extension-method shape (MapPublicArticles) is the convention every route group in the repository follows. PageSize is a constant, not a query-string parameter. An attacker-controlled page size is a denial-of-service lever, and a public feed has no reason to offer one. The filter form does send a max, so ParseTake falls back to PageSize for anything unparseable and caps the rest at 100. If you need paging, take a cursor, not a count. There is no RequireAuthorization, and that is the deliberate half. The GET changes nothing and shows only rows already marked published, so it needs no authentication and no antiforgery token. The admin routes under /admin sit behind sign-in and redirect an anonymous visitor to /signin; this one must not, and adding the guard here would be the thing to reconsider rather than omitting it. Two tests below exist solely to hold that line, because an absent guard is invisible in a diff. There is no output cache on this route either. The HTML embeds an antiforgery token for the filter form, so a stored copy would be served to a visitor whose cookie cannot match it. A page that carries no token can be cached; see AsPublicPage in PublicDocuments.cs. 5. The wiring One line in Program.cs, next to the generated routes it contrasts with: app.MapScaffolding(); app.MapPublicArticles(); and one registration per branch of the store choice, which is where the whole feature's persistence decision lives: // no connection string builder.Services.AddSingleton<IPublishedArticles>(new MemoryPublishedArticles(articles, tags, relation)); // with one builder.Services.AddSingleton<IPublishedArticles>( sp => new JauntyPublishedArticles(sp.GetRequiredService<NpgsqlDataSource>())); The choice is made in the application, not in a library, and it is made from ConnectionStrings:Sample. That key — and the environment-variable spelling ConnectionStrings__Sample the test tiers and CI use — is in configuration.md. 6. The unit tier AGENTS.md requires all three tiers for every feature, and the order in that requirement is not decorative: test each unit against its own inputs, failure paths included, and only then holistically through the real entry point. Both layers, not one standing in for the other. The unit tier carries the most facts here, and it is the only tier that can. It states one rule per test: Fact Rule it pins RecentAsync_OmitsUnpublishedArticles a draft is not public RecentAsync_OmitsArchivedArticles an archived row is not public either RecentAsync_OrdersByPublicationDate_BeforeCreationTime the publication date leads the order RecentAsync_DatesARowWithoutAPublicationDateByItsCreationDay the fallback date RecentAsync_OrdersNewestFirst seeded out of order, so passing cannot be insertion order RecentAsync_WithIdenticalTimestamps_BreaksTheTieById the tiebreak the SQL spells out RecentAsync_TakesAtMostTheRequestedCount_KeepingTheNewest truncation happens after the sort Results_WithNoArticles_SaysSoRatherThanRenderingAnEmptyList the empty state Results_WithAnArticle_ShowsTitleAuthorAndDate the happy path Results_WithNoAuthor_OmitsTheAuthorElementEntirely the nullable field Results_EncodesTitleAndAuthor the encoding IndexIsAWholeDocumentAndResultsIsAFragment the page has the chrome, the swap target does not IndexEchoesTheSubmittedTakeRatherThanAConstant the form shows the value that produced the list Matching_FindsAnAuthorAsWellAsATitle the filter does what its placeholder says The integration tier could only ever say the page came back 200 with the right words on it. Only here does a failure name the rule that broke rather than the page that did. The template tests call the generated Templates.Articles.* methods directly, for example Templates.Articles.Results.ToHtmlString(locale: Locales.En, Articles: [...]). JauntyPublishedArticles is absent from this tier on purpose. Its whole body is one generated call and a row-to-record loop, so a unit test of it would be a test of a mock. What it actually does is a claim about PostgreSQL, and it gets asserted against PostgreSQL one tier up. 7. The integration tier WebApplicationFactory against a real host, and this file has two halves because the feature has two implementations. What is unproven until here: that the route exists, that it needs no sign-in, and — for the JauntyQ half — that the SQL binds its parameter and applies its WHERE and ORDER BY the way the C# above it assumes. A LINQ expression and a SQL statement can agree in the unit tier and disagree against PostgreSQL. Three techniques from this file are worth taking with you. Arrange the negative case so a missing filter is loud. The draft row is inserted newer than the published one, so a missing WHERE published puts it first on the page rather than merely somewhere on it: await SampleTestDatabase.InsertAsync($\"{prefix}-p\", $\"{prefix}-published\", \"xunit\", \"body\", published: true, createdAt: future); await SampleTestDatabase.InsertAsync($\"{prefix}-d\", $\"{prefix}-draft\", \"xunit\", \"body\", published: false, createdAt: future.AddMinutes(1)); Date rows into the future, and remove them afterwards. The page shows the newest twenty and nativeweb_sample_test accumulates rows across runs, so a row dated NOW() is only reliably on the page while the suite is young. The suite does not truncate — every other test finds its row by unique title at any position, so accumulation is harmless there — but a future-dated row left behind would displace the next run's rows out of the page, and the suite would break itself after ten runs. Hence a finally that deletes exactly the ids this test minted: finally { await SampleTestDatabase.DeleteByIdPrefixAsync(prefix); } Assert the open route by requesting it. Feed_IsReachableWithoutSigningIn asks for a 200 and for <h1 class=\"page__title\">Articles<\/h1> in the body, with AllowAutoRedirect = false. Nothing but a request proves which side of the authentication line a route landed on. 8. The end-to-end tier Playwright, a real browser, the NativeAOT binary, real PostgreSQL. The usual argument for this tier is that only a browser can drive a dialog or a script, and this page has no JavaScript, so that argument does not apply. Two others do, and they are the ones to reason from when you are deciding whether your own feature needs this tier. It is the only place the feature runs inside a NativeAOT binary. Everything below it is a JIT-hosted test process. ListPublishedAsync is generated code doing reflection-free mapping and the compiled templates are generated code; either could work perfectly under the JIT and be trimmed out of the published binary. A green integration tier says nothing about that. A browser follows redirects, and the tier below it cannot. AllowAutoRedirect = false is the right way to observe a guard and the wrong way to observe its absence. Feed_IsReachedByABrowserWithNoSession checks the URL first, because a redirect to /signin would still render a page and an assertion on the heading alone would not notice which page. The encoding fact also changes meaning here. The tiers below check the HTML for &lt;script&gt;, which is a claim about a string. Only here is there a real HTML parser and a real script engine to be wrong about it: Assert.Equal(0, await page.Locator(\"li.articles__item script\").CountAsync()); Assert.False(await page.EvaluateAsync<bool>(\"() => window.__pwned === true\")); An injected script element would be in the DOM, and every string assertion below would have passed all the way up. 9. Perturb every green test AGENTS.md again, and it is the rule most easily skipped: a test that passes because the code under it never executed asserts nothing. When a new test passes on its first run, change the code so it should fail, and confirm it does. Perturb one thing at a time. Changing WHERE and ORDER BY together can fail more tests than you expected, and a perturbation that fails a test by an unintended route proves nothing about that test. Then read the failure message rather than counting failures: a test that goes red for the wrong reason is as useless as one that goes green for the wrong reason. For example, adding .RequireAuthorization() to this route fails the e2e guard test, and the message shows the browser followed /articles to /signin to /admin. Run it all dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.sample.tests.unit dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.scaffold.tests.integration dotnet test --project tests/nativeweb.scaffold.tests.e2e The integration and e2e tiers need nativeweb_sample_test — db/sample/README.md has the two lines that create it, and the Playwright browser install is in getting-started § 4. What to take to your own feature Narrow the read model to what the page renders. It is the cheapest defence against a UI model and a storage model becoming the same type. Put the filter and the sort where the data is. The in-memory implementation shows you what it costs when you cannot. Let the diagnostics stop you. JNT8007 is a question about your schema, not an obstacle; answer it with an index or with a reason, and never with a blanket suppression. Encode at the point of output, visibly. State each rule in the tier that can name it, and only then assert the whole page. Perturb it. One change at a time, and read the message. Reference material, once you need a specific answer rather than a path, is indexed in docs/README.md."},{"u":"01-architecture-native-web-architecture.html","t":"Lean Native Web (LNW) — Architecture Specification & Handoff","s":"Architecture","h":[{"i":"status","t":"Status"},{"i":"1-vision","t":"1. Vision"},{"i":"2-core-principles","t":"2. Core Principles"},{"i":"3-technology-stack","t":"3. Technology Stack"},{"i":"4-technologies-explicitly-avoided","t":"4. Technologies Explicitly Avoided"},{"i":"5-project-structure","t":"5. Project Structure"},{"i":"6-data-access--jauntyq","t":"6. Data Access — JauntyQ"},{"i":"7-dependency-injection","t":"7. Dependency Injection"},{"i":"8-where-htmx-goes-where-alpine-goes-where-neither-goes","t":"8. Where HTMX goes, where Alpine goes, where neither goes"},{"i":"9-web-components","t":"9. Web Components"},{"i":"10-authentication--authorization","t":"10. Authentication & Authorization"},{"i":"11-static-assets-serialization-configuration","t":"11. Static Assets, Serialization, Configuration"},{"i":"12-error-handling","t":"12. Error Handling"},{"i":"13-migration-from-svelte--deno-bff","t":"13. Migration from Svelte + Deno BFF"},{"i":"14-measured-benchmarks","t":"14. Measured Benchmarks"},{"i":"15-htmx-4","t":"15. htmx 4"},{"i":"16-handoff-checklist--the-original-open-risks-kept-as-a-record","t":"16. Handoff Checklist — the original open risks, kept as a record"}],"b":"Lean Native Web (LNW) — Architecture Specification & Handoff Status The sections below are the design; this block is what of it exists. Everything §2 states as a principle is enforced by something that fails when it is broken: Where Proved by Minimal APIs, server-rendered HTML libs/nativeweb.web, libs/nativeweb.render the test projects under tests/ NativeAOT, no trim warnings PublishAot, TreatWarningsAsErrors repo-wide the publish itself; a warning is an error Compile-time HTML, forms and CRUD the source generators under libs/ the generated code is the only code path; no runtime reflection outside the one assembly-attribute read behind /version JauntyQ for all data access db/sample, apps/sample wired and running, not a placeholder list HTMX + Alpine, vendored framework assets in libs/nativeweb.web/wwwroot/_nativeweb/; htmx and Alpine.js in apps/sample/wwwroot/vendor/ served from the app's own files, no CDN. Alpine.js is loaded but no page uses it yet The reference application apps/sample the only deployable project in the tree The template a product is stamped from skeleton/, tools/new-product.ps1 CI stamps and tests a product on every push Decided, and deliberately absent. Auth is not the framework's to pick, so §10's JWT-bearer default is a recommendation to a product rather than a description of this repo: apps/sample uses a cookie and a demo sign-in, and the framework registers no authentication scheme. libs/nativeweb.signin is a relying party (client) only; there is no identity provider in this repository (decision 007). The line NativeWeb holds is Django's, where contrib.auth ships and the OAuth2/OIDC server does not. §16's checklist is the original list of open risks, kept for the reasoning in it, not as a current to-do. 1. Vision Lean Native Web (LNW) is a server-first architecture for ASP.NET Core that prioritizes explicit code, NativeAOT compatibility, predictable performance, and long-term maintainability. It intentionally avoids unnecessary framework abstractions while keeping the mature parts of ASP.NET Core that are difficult or risky to replace. 2. Core Principles NativeAOT First. Do not add libraries or patterns that require runtime code generation, runtime reflection, assembly scanning, dynamic proxies, or plugin loading. Everything should be trim-safe and compile-time friendly. Server Owns the Application. The server owns business logic, routing, authentication, authorization, validation, HTML generation, and database access. The browser is a presentation layer. HTML First. Prefer HTML → Browser over JSON → JavaScript → HTML for the application's own UI. Use JSON only for public APIs, third-party integrations, mobile clients, and external consumers. Explicit Over Magic. Avoid assembly scanning, automatic registration, reflection-based discovery, and hidden conventions. Prefer explicit object creation, explicit endpoint registration, explicit routing, explicit configuration. Lean by Default. Every dependency must justify its existence. Do not introduce abstraction unless it solves a demonstrated problem. 3. Technology Stack Backend: .NET 10, C# 14, ASP.NET Core Minimal APIs, NativeAOT, System.Text.Json source generation Frontend: HTMX, Alpine.js, static HTML/CSS, optional Web Components (Lit, or Svelte compiled to a CSR-only custom element — never Svelte SSR; see §13) Database: SQLite for development, PostgreSQL for production Data access: JauntyQ (§6) — not Dapper, not Dapper.AOT, not EF Core Authentication: ASP.NET Core Authentication, JWT Bearer as the default (§10) Authorization: ASP.NET Core Authorization Policies 4. Technologies Explicitly Avoided Unless there is a documented, specific reason: MVC, Controllers, Razor Pages, Blazor (Server or WASM) — Razor's runtime view compilation and Blazor Server's SignalR-circuit model are not what NativeAOT's Minimal-API path was built around; confirmed unsupported for Native AOT as of .NET 10/11 MediatR, AutoMapper — indirection with no demonstrated problem behind it here Repository Pattern as IRepository<T> — a named, per-aggregate class (TodoRepository) is fine; a generic interface forcing one shape onto every aggregate is not (JauntyQ's .sql-per-query model makes this doubly true — a generic repository would fight the whole point of hand-written, validated SQL per query) Dapper — vanilla Dapper maps rows to objects using runtime reflection and Reflection.Emit-generated IL, which directly violates principle 1. Dapper.AOT fixes this via build-time interceptors, but that's a second package bolted on to reach a bar JauntyQ already clears natively, with no interceptor step required EF Core — not Native AOT-compatible; this is a hard blocker, not a style preference Assembly scanning, reflection-based serializers, runtime code generation, Service Locator, dynamic plugins 5. Project Structure LeanNativeWeb/ ├── Program.cs ├── LeanNativeWeb.csproj ├── appsettings.json │ ├── Endpoints/ one static class per feature (Home.cs, Auth.cs, Todo.cs, Admin.cs) ├── Render/ HTML-returning functions per feature, no template engine required ├── Queries/ JauntyQ .sql files, one per query, versioned alongside the code │ that calls them (see §6) ├── Auth/ ├── Models/ └── wwwroot/ ├── vendor/ htmx, Alpine.js and fonts, vendored, not CDN-loaded ├── css/ └── components/ hand-written or Lit-based Web Components (see §9) Organize by feature, not by technical layer — Endpoints/, Render/, and Queries/ each hold one file per feature, not one folder per feature containing mixed concerns. A single Auth/ folder is the one deliberate exception, since authentication is infrastructure shared across every feature, not a feature itself. Each endpoint should: receive the request, validate input, execute business logic, return TypedResults. TypedResults (not the untyped Results helpers) is the AOT- and analyzer-friendly choice — it gives OpenAPI/analyzer tooling real compile-time type information instead of an IResult box. Simple request flow: Endpoint → JauntyQ → HTML. Not Controller → Service → Repository → Mapper → DTO → Database. 6. Data Access — JauntyQ JauntyQ is a compile-time SQL-to-C# source generator: you write real SQL in versioned .sql files; at build time JauntyQ parses them against a committed schema snapshot and emits typed C# methods. If a column is renamed or a table drops, the build breaks, not production. The generated code is performance-aligned by construction: Column values are read by ordinal (GetInt32(0), GetString(1)), never by name. Parameters are bound with explicit DbType from the schema — no provider type inference at runtime. No reflection, zero per-row boxing, no runtime SQL parsing. (Parameter values box once per call at the ADO.NET boundary — DbParameter.Value is object, a fixed cost every data library pays regardless of ORM. Once per call, not once per row, which is why it does not scale with result size.) A one-time shape guard validates result-set column names and count on the first call per query, then never again. Because there's no reflection and no runtime code generation, the emitted code is Native AOT and trim compatible by construction — no separate .AOT package, no interceptor step, unlike Dapper. Extrode.JauntyQ.Runtime is marked IsAotCompatible, and samples/JauntyQ.Aot.Smoke publishes to a self-contained native binary — verified in CI — running the full auto-CRUD, identity-return, and @stream paths against SQLite. The database provider must also be AOT-friendly — the ORM being AOT-safe doesn't make the driver underneath it AOT-safe automatically: Development (SQLite): Microsoft.Data.Sqlite.Core + SQLitePCLRaw.bundle_e_sqlite3 Production (PostgreSQL): Npgsql, confirmed fully NativeAOT/trimming-compatible since 8.0 — a deliberate, large rewrite of the driver's internals, current as of Npgsql 10.0. Use Npgsql directly, not through EF Core's Npgsql provider (EF Core itself still isn't AOT-compatible, so the moment EF Core is in the graph, AOT is off the table regardless of which database driver sits underneath it). 7. Dependency Injection Minimize the built-in container. Prefer direct object construction: var jaunty = new JauntyContext(connectionString); var jwt = new JwtService(secret); instead of builder.Services.AddSingleton<JauntyContext>(); The container should only be used where framework integrations require it — authentication middleware being the clearest example (§10). Where this needs a caveat the principle doesn't state on its own: this is directionally supported by what was actually measured in this process — stripping DI (along with routing and middleware) going from Minimal APIs to raw Kestrel produced a real ~1.8–2.2x throughput gain (§13) — but that experiment changed DI, routing, and middleware together, so \"DI specifically, holding routing constant\" was never isolated as its own variable. Two costs the principle doesn't weigh: scoped lifetimes (a per-request JauntyQ connection/ transaction is exactly where a container earns its keep for free; hand-rolling that lifetime via closures or HttpContext.Items is very doable but is ceremony DI would otherwise absorb) and testability (substituting a test double is trivial with container-based injection and comparatively awkward with closures over manually constructed singletons). Minimizing DI is a reasonable default here; treating it as costless in every case is not. 8. Where HTMX goes, where Alpine goes, where neither goes The heuristic: HTMX when the server has the answer. Alpine when the state is purely local and the server never needs to know it. An island (Lit, or Svelte compiled to a CSR-only custom element, no SSR) when you need real client-side reactive computation over data already on the page. Interaction Tool Why Add / edit / delete / list, navigation (hx-boost), search/filter/sort, live-updating regions, infinite scroll HTMX Server owns the data; a round trip is the cheapest model to reason about Modal visibility, dropdowns, tabs, accordions, a \"new item\" form toggle Alpine Pure ephemeral view state, never persisted, server never needs it Computed preview (subtotal, char counter), a handful of interdependent form fields Alpine Needs to feel instant; manageable in x-data without a round trip Inline edit-in-place HTMX, by default See note below — this is not as clean a case as it first looks Drag-and-drop with live visual feedback, canvas editor, spreadsheet-like grid Island Needs continuous client state neither htmx nor Alpine is built for Form logic that's outgrown x-data (rule of thumb: >~15 lines) Island Sign of rebuilding a reactive framework badly in an attribute A note on inline editing specifically, because it's genuinely a compound interaction and worth getting right rather than filing under a single tool by reflex: the well-known, idiomatic htmx \"click to edit\" pattern handles the whole thing as two server round trips — one hx-get for the edit-mode fragment, one hx-post/hx-put to save and return the display-mode fragment — with zero client-side state at any point. That's the default here, because it's the version most consistent with principle 2 (server owns the application): the server never has to trust a client-computed value it hasn't validated, and there's nothing to keep in sync. Routing the display↔edit toggle through Alpine instead is legitimate only when the round-trip latency for that toggle specifically is a demonstrated problem — it's the exception, not the default, and it should be a deliberate choice, not a reflex. Anti-patterns: Don't mirror server state in Alpine. The rendered DOM (from htmx) is the state. A separate x-data array duplicating what htmx already fetched is two sources of truth. Don't force a round trip for something obviously local \"for consistency.\" Don't let one Alpine component's x-data grow past ~15 lines — reach for an island. Don't rewrite a working Svelte component into htmx+Alpine mid-migration if it's genuinely rich-interaction — recompile it as a CSR custom element instead (§12.6). 9. Web Components Two distinct, non-conflicting uses: Reuse and encapsulation — Navbar, Sidebar, Dialog, Toast, Avatar, Loading Spinner, Theme Toggle: shared UI repeated across many server-rendered pages, worth encapsulating once. These can be hand-written vanilla Custom Elements or Lit — either way, no build step is required if shipped as native ES modules. Rich-interaction islands (§8's \"Island\" row) — genuinely stateful widgets where htmx/Alpine would mean fighting the tool. Do not create business-domain components — CustomerList, InvoicePage, OrdersScreen. Those belong on the server; a component-per-page here is just an SPA wearing a Web Components costume, undermining principle 2 entirely. Prefer Lit over Svelte for anything in category 1: Lit ships as plain ES modules and needs no compile step, keeping \"no build pipeline unless absolutely necessary\" (§11) intact. Reach for Svelte-as-custom-element only for category 2, and only compiled customElement: true, never SSR (§12.6) — SSR is the one thing that reintroduces the Node/Deno dependency this whole architecture exists to avoid. 10. Authentication & Authorization Always use ASP.NET Core's built-in authentication and authorization. Never manually implement JWT parsing, signature verification, cookie encryption, or claims validation — keep Microsoft's security implementation; this is the sharpest exception to §7's DI-minimization stance, and the framework registration this needs is exactly the \"required by framework integrations\" case §7 already carves out. JWT Bearer is the default here, not \"JWT Bearer or Cookie Authentication\" as interchangeable options — they are not equivalent under NativeAOT. JWT bearer authentication is confirmed AOT-clean. Cookie and OIDC handlers have shown up as unsupported in NativeAOT compatibility tables historically. If cookie-based auth is a hard requirement, verify it directly: publish with PublishAot=true against the actual auth configuration and read the trim-analyzer output before committing, rather than assuming either way. Use Authorization Policies (.RequireAuthorization(\"Admin\")) over manual claim inspection. 11. Static Assets, Serialization, Configuration Static assets: wwwroot/, vendored htmx/Alpine, no CDN dependency, no npm chain for a simple application. Use MapStaticAssets (not UseStaticFiles) for build-time compression and content-hash fingerprinting. Serialization: always source-generated System.Text.Json (a JsonSerializerContext with [JsonSerializable] per DTO). Reflection-based serialization is prohibited. Configuration: strongly typed configuration objects, loaded once, not IConfiguration injected throughout. One thing worth being explicit about, since it's easy to get half-right: IConfiguration.Bind()/.Get<T>() are reflection-based by default and throw real IL2026/IL3050 trim warnings under AOT. Set <EnableConfigurationBindingGenerator>true<\/EnableConfigurationBindingGenerator> explicitly to get the source-generated, reflection-free path — the same category of gap as Dapper vs. JauntyQ: the destination is right, the mechanism that gets you there under NativeAOT has to be turned on, it isn't automatic. 12. Error Handling Centralized middleware. Two different response shapes for two different consumers, decided deliberately rather than defaulted into one: JSON API routes (external/mobile/third-party): ProblemDetails, RFC 7807, appropriate HTTP status codes. HTML/htmx routes: a real HTML fragment shaped for the target it'll be swapped into — not ProblemDetails JSON, which would render as a broken blob of text in the DOM. This distinction matters: htmx 4 swaps 4xx/5xx responses into the target by default. The layouts set htmx-config noSwap to [204, 304, \"4xx\", \"5xx\"], so a guard rejection (415, 400 antiforgery) does not render its body inside the page — see §15. Decide the error-fragment shape for htmx routes, but don't leave it undecided in a codebase that serves both response types from the same host. Avoid exception-driven business logic; logging stays on the built-in ILogger, added only where needed. 13. Migration from Svelte + Deno BFF The reassuring part first: the C# NativeAOT API layer doesn't need to change. This migration replaces the BFF + frontend layer; the domain/service logic underneath is reused close to as-is, which drastically lowers the risk relative to a full rewrite. Classify every existing screen against §8's table: CRUD-shaped → port to htmx; genuinely rich-interaction → keep the Svelte component, change only delivery (§13.6). Stand up the new ASP.NET Core service alongside the running system. Reuse the existing C# domain/service classes directly. Re-home auth into ASP.NET Core directly (§10) — verify AOT compatibility against the real scheme in use, don't assume. Strangler-fig cutover, one route at a time, behind a reverse proxy (Caddy, or YARP to stay in .NET) — fits an Incus/LXC setup directly: new service in its own container, a small proxy container path-routing between old and new. Data layer is untouched. Same Postgres/SQLite, same schema — this migration doesn't touch data at all. For screens staying rich-interaction: recompile, don't rewrite. Drop the SSR compile target, add customElement: true (<svelte:options customElement={{ tag: \"...\", shadow: \"none\" }} />), point data fetching directly at the C# Minimal API (no more BFF to proxy through), build once with Vite, ship the output as a static file next to htmx.min.js. Validate each cut-over route before the next — before/after latency with wrk/k6 (§14), plus a Playwright smoke pass. Retire Deno once everything's ported — decommission the BFF process and the SvelteKit SSR build entirely. 14. Measured Benchmarks Not a projection — a minimal but complete version of both stacks was built (same conceptual todo functionality, JSON on one side and HTML fragments on the other) and load-tested with wrk, three runs per test, a fixed 15-item dataset throughout (a growing dataset inflates repeated-write numbers). Single-vCPU sandbox — absolute numbers are a floor, not what real server hardware shows, but the relative comparison holds regardless of hardware. req/s (avg of 3 runs) avg latency C# JSON API direct (the floor, no BFF at all) 26,233 2.0ms HTMX + ASP.NET Core — GET / (full page) 21,757 2.4ms HTMX + ASP.NET Core — POST toggle 18,624 2.7ms Svelte+Deno — POST toggle (Deno→C# API hop, no render) 3,398 14.3ms Svelte+Deno — SSR GET / (full page) 1,578 34ms Where the cost comes from, isolated: the Deno→C# hop alone (a mutation, no rendering) is ~5.5x slower than doing the same thing in-process in ASP.NET Core; Svelte's SSR rendering on top of that hop costs another ~2.2x — SSR, not the network hop, is the more expensive half. End to end: HTMX+ASP.NET Core is ~13.8x faster than Svelte+Deno for the same page, ~16.6x faster than the theoretical C#-API-only floor. Memory (RSS) under load: htmx-skeleton 49MB, C# JSON API 49.6MB, the Deno process 186MB — 3.8x either C# process. V8 warm-up, caught without a dedicated test for it: the SSR runs climbed 1,291 → 1,651 → 1,793 req/s across three back-to-back runs on the same still-warming process. NativeAOT has no equivalent curve. A separate, narrower experiment (§ raw Kestrel vs. Minimal API, not part of the recommendation): stripping ASP.NET Core down to raw Kestrel — no routing engine, no DI, no middleware — measured ~1.8–2.2x higher throughput and ~28% less idle memory than Minimal APIs. Real, but not adopted as the default: it costs the \"boring, AI/human-legible, huge corpus\" property that makes Minimal APIs the maintainability winner, and per §7, \"DI alone\" was never isolated as the specific variable responsible for the gain. These figures are frozen. They came from a purpose-built pair of minimal apps load-tested with wrk in a single-vCPU sandbox. Neither app is in this repo, so nothing can reproduce them and nothing should be compared against them. They stand as the relative comparison they were made for: HTMX+ASP.NET Core against Svelte+Deno, ~13.8x on the same page. Not as a target. For regression work, use tools/bench/. A Deno load driver that measures apps/sample against a committed baseline.json; --check exits 1 on a regression (20% down on throughput, 25% up on RSS). The baseline is only comparable on the machine and harness settings it was recorded with. tools/bench/README.md has the commands and exit codes. The htmx 4 upgrade changed the client asset: the minified library went from 51,238 B to 36,716 B, a 28.3% reduction. It does not move a server-side throughput number: htmx runs in the browser and does not appear in the request path either harness measures. 15. htmx 4 htmx isn't a package dependency here: one vendored file (wwwroot/vendor/htmx/htmx.min.js in apps/sample and in the skeleton) referenced from a single <script src>, plus hx-* attribute strings inside templates. The vendored version is htmx 4, which ships under the next dist-tag while 2.x holds latest. The previous 2.x file is kept at libs/nativeweb.assets/rollback/htmx-2.0.10.min.js, outside every wwwroot so a superseded library is not served or shipped in the published output. Rollback is copying it over the vendored htmx.min.js files. How the codebase meets htmx 4's changed defaults: No implicit inheritance. Every hx-* attribute is derived by boot.js onto the same element that carries the data-nw-*, so no parent configures a descendant and :inherited is needed nowhere. implicitInheritance stays at its false default. 4xx/5xx are not swapped. noSwap takes an array defaulting to [204, 304]; the matcher tests the status against \"404\", \"40x\" and \"4xx\" in turn, so the value is [204, 304, \"4xx\", \"5xx\"], not a bare ['4xx','5xx']. Validation errors return 200 OK with a fragment and are unaffected; the 4xx paths are guards (415 content-type, 400 antiforgery), whose bodies would otherwise render inside the page. htmx 4 also has a per-element hx-status:4xx attribute if a finer rule is wanted. No hx-boost. Nothing in the templates uses it. hx-disable means something else. In htmx 4 it means \"disable this control during the request\" (htmx 2's hx-disabled-elt), and htmx 2's \"don't process this subtree\" moved to hx-ignore. Same attribute name, different job, no error if you get it wrong. Timeouts. NativeWebOptions.RequestTimeout defaults to 30 s, inside htmx 4's 60,000 ms defaultTimeout, so no allowance is set. No compat shim. implicitInheritance: true is not used. What to watch for in an upgrade: not the renames. Three behaviours changed under the same-looking API. after:request stopped covering error/timeout/abort (restore moved to finally:request), after:swap moved its dispatch from the swapped-in element to the issuing one, and every payload moved from flat detail.* fields onto detail.ctx. The first two fail silently — a mechanical rename leaves a permanently disabled button and a focus bug, both green in CI. Watch, don't act on yet: htmx 4's declarative signals may let some of §8's Alpine rows move to plain htmx, and a proposed hx-drag extension could shrink the \"Island\" row for simple drag-and-drop cases. The hx-alpine-compat extension is not used. 16. Handoff Checklist — the original open risks, kept as a record This list is historical, not a to-do. It was written before any of this existed. Most of it has since been answered by building the thing: the AOT publish runs in CI against the real dependency graph, JauntyQ drives apps/sample and db/sample, and there is no strangler-fig window because there was no legacy stack to strangle. It is kept for the reasoning inside each item, which still applies. Auth AOT compatibility for whatever scheme is actually configured (§10) — the one item in this spec not yet verified against a real setup. Every NuGet dependency the real application will carry — re-run the AOT publish against the actual dependency graph, not the toy skeleton's. JauntyQ + Npgsql/ Microsoft.Data.Sqlite are confirmed clean paths (§6); anything added beyond those needs its own check. Session/auth state during the strangler-fig window (§13) — if a session must survive a request being routed to a different backend mid-session, the session store needs to be readable by both stacks during the transition. Confirm the config-binding source generator and TypedResults are actually used in the real codebase, not just specified here (§5, §11) — both are opt-in, not automatic."},{"u":"01-architecture-progressive-enhancement.html","t":"Lean Native Web: Progressive Enhancement Layer","s":"Architecture","h":[{"i":"philosophy","t":"Philosophy"},{"i":"the-enhancement-layer-bootjs","t":"The enhancement layer: boot.js"},{"i":"attribute-conventions","t":"Attribute conventions"},{"i":"core-rules","t":"Core rules"},{"i":"post-only-endpoints","t":"POST-only endpoints"},{"i":"html-ownership","t":"HTML ownership"},{"i":"built-in-ux-defaults","t":"Built-in UX defaults"},{"i":"busy-states","t":"Busy states"},{"i":"focus-after-swap","t":"Focus after swap"},{"i":"flash-messages","t":"Flash messages"},{"i":"keyboard","t":"Keyboard"},{"i":"validation","t":"Validation"},{"i":"version-and-error-pages","t":"/version and error pages"},{"i":"browser-native-first","t":"Browser-native first"},{"i":"any-content-type","t":"Any content type"},{"i":"considered-and-rejected-from-the-v2-handoff","t":"Considered and rejected (from the v2 handoff)"},{"i":"guarantee","t":"Guarantee"}],"b":"Lean Native Web: Progressive Enhancement Layer The frontend enhancement conventions for Lean Native Web. Mandatory for all browser-facing pages in every LNW app. Attributes are data-nw-*, endpoints are POST-only, and boot.js is the only enhancement mechanism. Philosophy Every page must be fully functional without JavaScript. JavaScript exists solely to progressively enhance the user experience. Applications must never require JavaScript for authentication, login, logout, CRUD, forms, navigation, search, or pagination. Without JavaScript: the application remains completely usable — all forms submit normally, all links navigate normally, all actions complete. With JavaScript: htmx upgrades interactions so pages avoid full reloads. Only the user experience changes; business logic never changes. Build order, always: semantic HTML → server functionality → HTMX enhancement → Alpine.js enhancement. Never reverse it. The enhancement layer: boot.js boot.js lives in libs/nativeweb.web/wwwroot/_nativeweb/ and reaches every app through libs/nativeweb.web/build/NativeWeb.Web.targets, which serves it at /_nativeweb/boot.js. It is loaded with defer before htmx, so its normalization runs before htmx processes the document. Its only responsibility is an HTML normalization pass: Detect elements that opt in with a data-nw-* attribute. Convert those semantic attributes into the matching hx-* attributes. Derive the htmx transport from the HTML that already works without JavaScript. It is not a frontend framework and not a rendering engine. It never renders business UI, never stores application state, never duplicates server validation, never replaces server routing. Two hooks cover every element: a DOMContentLoaded sweep for the initial document, and an htmx:before:process listener for fragments swapped in by htmx. Normalization is idempotent. htmx 4 dispatches htmx:before:process and only then queries the subtree, so attributes added by the sweep are picked up by the very pass that follows. boot.js needs no htmx.process() re-submission. Attribute conventions An element opts in by carrying any data-nw-* attribute. The vocabulary: You write boot.js sets Meaning (htmx semantics) data-nw-target=\"#list\" hx-target=\"#list\" where the response fragment lands data-nw-swap=\"outerHTML\" hx-swap=\"outerHTML\" how the fragment is swapped in data-nw-trigger=\"revealed\" hx-trigger=\"revealed\" what event fires the request data-nw-confirm=\"Delete?\" hx-confirm=\"Delete?\" confirm dialog before the request Transport is never written by hand — it comes from the element itself: <form method=\"post\" action=\"/x\" data-nw-…> → hx-post=\"/x\"; any other method → hx-get. <a href=\"/x\" data-nw-…> → hx-get=\"/x\". A form with a missing or empty action gets no derived transport — htmx resolves an empty path against the base URL and merges query params where native submission replaces them, so the two paths would diverge. Enhanced forms always carry an explicit action. Example: <form method=\"post\" action=\"/bookmarks\" data-nw-target=\"#list\" data-nw-swap=\"beforeend\"> Without JavaScript the form POSTs and the server redirects (Post/Redirect/Get). With JavaScript, boot.js adds hx-post=\"/bookmarks\" hx-target=\"#list\" hx-swap=\"beforeend\" and htmx takes over. Core rules Opt-in only. Elements without a data-nw-* attribute are never touched. There is no automatic boosting of plain links or forms. Authored hx-* always wins. boot.js never overwrites an existing hx-* attribute — per attribute, not per element. Developers may always use htmx directly; data-nw-* is an optional convenience, never a replacement. Sources stay. data-nw-* attributes are never removed or altered, so the served HTML stays valid and the enhancement remains inspectable. POST-only endpoints State changes are POST to a verb-named route — one route, one handler, one test surface: POST /todos/5/delete There are no parallel REST routes (DELETE /todos/5), no data-nw-method, and no virtual PUT/PATCH/DELETE. A browser without JavaScript can only GET and POST; a second spelling of the same action would double the endpoint surface and its tests to serve requests the enhanced path also makes as POST. Endpoints inspect the HX-Request header: an htmx request gets an HTML fragment (or HX-Redirect); anything else gets a complete page or a normal HTTP redirect. Business logic is identical on both paths. HTML ownership The server owns HTML generation. htmx only transports HTML. Alpine.js only manages local UI state (menus, toggles, purely visual concerns). Business state never lives in JavaScript. Built-in UX defaults The layer every LNW app gets for free, on top of normalization. It ships in the sample app and the skeleton. Busy states boot.js owns in-flight feedback: on htmx:before:request it resolves the triggering submit control (from ctx.request.submitter) and sets disabled, aria-busy=\"true\" and class nw-busy; on htmx:finally:request it restores, and re-focuses the control when the swap dropped focus to <body>. No per-form markup, no hx-indicator repetition; each app styles .nw-busy in its own components.css. Accepted posture: busy treatment covers submit controls only — links and background triggers stay unstyled. Restore is on finally:, not after:. htmx 4's after:request fires only once the response body has been read and is cancelable, so a dropped connection skips it entirely — restoring there would leave the submit control disabled with no way back. finally:request sits in the request's actual finally block and covers every path, including a before:request that another listener cancelled.. Focus after swap boot.js recovers focus only when it saw the swap take it: at htmx:before:swap it records whether the focused element (or the busy-disabled submitter, whose disabled is what drops focus to <body>) sits inside the swap target; on htmx:after:swap with that record set it focuses the first [autofocus] inside the swapped content, else the swapped root via tabindex=\"-1\". A polling or load-triggered swap that fires while focus is already on <body> deliberately does not autofocus, and focus that survived the swap is never stolen. htmx 4 dispatches after:swap on ctx.sourceElement — the element that issued the request — so event.target may not be in the swapped region at all. boot.js reads the live content through event.detail.ctx.target. Reading event.target fails silently: focus lands somewhere plausible and accessibility regresses with nothing erroring. Layouts must not carry their own after:swap autofocus script — boot.js is the single owner. Flash messages Flash.Set(context, kind, message) before a success redirect writes a __Host-nw-flash cookie (\"ok:\"/\"err:\" prefix, escaped, 300-char cap with surrogate-safe truncation, 5-minute MaxAge); the next full-page render takes it read-once (expiring the cookie in the same response) and shows the standard banner. One mechanism covers both transports because success navigation is already unified: a real redirect no-JS, HX-Redirect enhanced — either way the next request renders the full layout. Flash lives in libs/nativeweb.web; the take happens explicitly at full-page render sites, never in a fragment path, or a fragment render would consume the flash invisibly. Accepted posture: under same-site prerender a flash can double-show or be consumed silently — cosmetic, accepted. Keyboard Skip link first in the tab order, main tabindex=\"-1\", :focus-visible rings. An app may add access keys on its primary actions, carried as accesskey + aria-keyshortcuts + the combo in title. Validation Server validation is authoritative, always; the browser's native layer mirrors it for free. Form fields carry required, minlength/maxlength/pattern/inputmode matching the server rules. Server-side failures render field-level errors: the input gets aria-invalid=\"true\" and aria-describedby=\"{id}-error\" (chaining any always-on hint), and an adjacent <p class=\"field__hint field__hint--error\" id=\"{id}-error\" role=\"alert\"> carries the message. Non-field failures keep the page banner. Boundary: sign-in and other credential failures stay generic banners — enumeration defense forbids saying which part was wrong; integration tests pin this. /version and error pages Designed error pages (400–500 range) via ErrorPageMiddleware + StaticErrorShell, HX-aware. /version beside /healthz returns InformationalVersion (<Version>, plus a short git SHA with a \"dev\" fallback where the product's csproj carries the StampSourceRevision MSBuild target, as the skeleton's does), unauthenticated, in robots.txt disallow. Accepted posture: the SHA is disclosed deliberately — it identifies a public commit, not a secret. Browser-native first Before reaching for JavaScript or a library, check whether the browser already ships the feature. Prefer <dialog>, <details>/<summary>, popover, <template>, native form validation, lazy loading, and view transitions over rebuilding them; do not build replacements for browser capabilities. Any content type LNW apps answer the web's text formats through one typed helper each, and offer several representations of one route through one mapping call (decision 009). One call per format. FormatResults in libs/nativeweb.render has Text, Markdown, Json (source-generated JsonTypeInfo<T> only, so it trims), Xml (an XmlWriter callback), Csv (RFC 4180, CRLF, formula-neutralising), Atom, Rss and Sitemap (structured input, absolute links resolved against the request's public base). Every helper buffers, declares Content-Length and writes once, for the throughput reason TemplateResults.Html records. One route, several representations. MapNegotiated(\"/articles\", [Html, Json, Csv], ...) in libs/nativeweb.web maps /articles and /articles.{format} to one handler that is told which format was chosen. Precedence is the extension, then Accept by quality and specificity, then the first offer. An extension the route does not offer is a 404 and an Accept that admits nothing is a 406, both text/plain naming the offers and neither reaching the handler. The extensionless route says Vary: Accept on every response. A page is cached only when every render is the same page. apps/sample declares /, /tags, /tags/{slug}, /feed, /sitemap.xml, /robots.txt and /about with AsPublicPage and leaves /articles/{slug} out of the cache on purpose: that page counts a view per render, and a cached copy would count one visit a minute. The three 404s an article slug can earn — unknown, unpublished, malformed — are one response, so the page never says which. The cache keys on the choice. PublicPageCachePolicy runs the same chooser and varies its key by the chosen media type, and refuses to store a response whose Content-Type is not the chosen format's. Two cookie-free representations of one path each get their own entry. What stays on the raw primitives. Bytes, streams and any body that cannot know its length stay on Results.Bytes and Results.Stream, as favicon does. What a sitemap lists and what robots.txt disallows are the application's, because the framework owns no route inventory; apps/sample/PublicDocuments.cs is the model. Considered and rejected (from the v2 handoff) Recorded so they aren't re-proposed: Bare attribute mapping (target= → hx-target=): rejected — target is a real HTML attribute with its own meaning; the data-nw-* prefix keeps served HTML valid and the opt-in explicit. data-lnw-method + dual routes (canonical DELETE /x plus fallback POST /x/delete): rejected — doubles the endpoint surface and its tests; POST-only verb routes are the convention (see above). Automatic #errors container: rejected — errors belong beside the field or in the page's own banner slot; a global sink invites blind innerHTML and detaches messages from their inputs. Route-generation DSL (<a route=\"Todo.Details\">): rejected — hardcoded hrefs plus the Hrefs helper class per app are simpler and visible in the served HTML. RequestContext wrapper: rejected — the per-request Chrome/FormChrome values built at render sites already carry what templates need without a context object drifting toward a service locator. Strongly-typed IDs: deferred, not rejected — waiting on JauntyQ converter support. Resource-oriented Features/ folders: rejected — the sample and the skeleton already follow the flat apps//libs//tests/ layout with per-area endpoint files; a restructure buys navigation churn, not clarity. Fragment URLs (GET /todos:list) as first-class outputs: rejected as a URL scheme — the goal (one endpoint serving page and fragment) already holds via the HX-Request header switch; a second URL per resource doubles the route surface. Html.Page / Html.Fragment rendering abstraction: rejected — the source-generated template Result(...) calls are already typed, AOT-safe and layout-aware; a stringly-named renderer would trade compile-time checking for indirection. \"Keep the framework small\" prohibition list (no custom ORM, migrations, auth, DI, template language, state management, JS framework): adopted in substance — it restates the stack rules in AGENTS.md and docs/constitution.md, which remain the binding record. The v2 items that were adopted — busy states, flash, native validation, browser-native-first, error pages, /version, asset versioning, CSP nonces (the latter two already existed) — live in the sections above. Guarantee Every feature must satisfy: works without JavaScript; works with JavaScript; htmx only improves responsiveness; HTML remains valid; browser behavior remains standards-compliant; no duplicated business logic. The JS-off e2e suites are the enforcement, not a review checklist."},{"u":"02-reference-breaking-changes.html","t":"Breaking changes","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"2026-10-20--the-frameworks-package-ids-gain-the-extrode-prefix","t":"2026-10-20 — the framework's package ids gain the Extrode. prefix"},{"i":"2026-09-05--four-shared-templates-are-delivered-by-the-framework-not-vendored","t":"2026-09-05 — four shared templates are delivered by the framework, not vendored"},{"i":"2026-09-01--iscaffoldstoretlistasync-takes-an-ordering-and-a-cursor","t":"2026-09-01 — IScaffoldStore<T>.ListAsync takes an ordering and a cursor"},{"i":"what-a-consumer-does","t":"What a consumer does"},{"i":"2026-08-31--the-identity-provider-sign-in-library-removed","t":"2026-08-31 — the identity-provider sign-in library removed"},{"i":"2026-08-31--libsnativewebauth-and-libsnativewebdata-removed","t":"2026-08-31 — libs/nativeweb.auth and libs/nativeweb.data removed"}],"b":"Breaking changes Every change that makes a consumer's existing code stop compiling or stop behaving as it did, newest first. A consumer reads this file top-down from wherever they last upgraded and stops when they reach an entry they have already taken. Entries are dated. Every entry below predates the first release, so none carries a version. Entries from the first v* tag onward name the version they first shipped in as well. What a version promises is in versioning.md — what each component means, what counts as breaking, how long a released minor is supported, and the deprecation path a library takes before it is removed. A breaking change is not a defect. This repository would rather break a seam than carry a shim that makes the wrong shape survivable, because a shim outlives the reason for it and the next reader cannot tell which of the two overloads is the one to write against. What is owed instead is this file: what changed, why, and the before/after a consumer edits by. 2026-10-20 — the framework's package ids gain the Extrode. prefix Seam: every PackageId under libs/ — NativeWeb.Render is now Extrode.NativeWeb.Render, and the same for the other twelve. Affects: a product building in package mode (-p:NativeWebUsePackages=true). Checkout mode references projects, not ids, and changes nothing. Namespaces and assembly names are unchanged, so no using line moves. The packages publish to nuget.org, alongside JauntyQ's Extrode.JauntyQ.*. No version has been released under either id, so there is nothing to restore from the old one. Rename the ids in Directory.Packages.props and in each package-mode PackageReference: <!-- before --> <PackageVersion Include=\"NativeWeb.Render\" Version=\"0.1.0\" /> <!-- after --> <PackageVersion Include=\"Extrode.NativeWeb.Render\" Version=\"0.1.0\" /> A stamped product's nuget.config no longer declares the framework-github source; nuget.org alone serves every pin. Delete the source, its packageSourceMapping entry and any framework-github credential in a user-level config. 2026-09-05 — four shared templates are delivered by the framework, not vendored Seam: NativeWeb.Web's MSBuild contribution — libs/nativeweb.web/build/NativeWeb.Web.targets. Affects: every product carrying its own templates/shared/antiforgery.html, bot-traps.html, banner-ok.html or banner-error.html. A product carrying none of them takes this change by rebuilding and changes nothing. These four render framework markup from framework code: Chrome.For fills the antiforgery input and issues the bot-trap canary, and Flash.Take feeds the two banners. As per-product copies they were a fix that could not ship. bot-traps.html was the sharp end — it spells five BotTraps field names as literal text, and a renamed constant left every vendored copy rendering fields the inspector no longer reads, with no compile error, no test failure and no runtime error. Bot detection just stopped. They now live at libs/nativeweb.web/templates/shared/ and are contributed as AdditionalFiles by NativeWeb.Web.targets. The logical names are unchanged — the render generator keys a template's name on the part after the last /templates/ segment of its physical path — so no call site changes: {{> shared/bot-traps Canary=BotCanary}} resolves as it always did. Delete your copy. That is the whole upgrade: rm templates/shared/antiforgery.html templates/shared/bot-traps.html rm templates/shared/banner-ok.html templates/shared/banner-error.html Leaving one in place is a build error naming the file, not a silent last-wins — the generator keys its model dictionary on the logical path, so the second file to arrive replaced the first, and which one that was depended on MSBuild item order. Two escape hatches, if deleting is not what you want: <!-- keep your own copy of one, and say so --> <ItemGroup> <NativeWebTemplateOverride Include=\"shared\\bot-traps.html\" /> <\/ItemGroup> <!-- take none of them --> <PropertyGroup> <NativeWebImportTemplates>false<\/NativeWebImportTemplates> <\/PropertyGroup> An override logs a message naming what it replaced. Turning the import off entirely makes a call site to a framework template fail at compile time with NWT005, which is the right failure. 2026-09-01 — IScaffoldStore<T>.ListAsync takes an ordering and a cursor Seam: NativeWeb.Scaffold.IScaffoldStore<T> — libs/nativeweb.scaffold/IScaffoldStore.cs. Affects: every hand-written store. Nothing generated changes: the generated endpoints, forms and routes are emitted against the new signature and a consumer regenerates by rebuilding. Offset paging is gone from the seam and from the generated list. A scaffolded list could not be ordered at all, and it paged by skip/take, which means a row inserted ahead of a reader pushes one row from the page they are on to the page they are about to request and they never see it. // before ValueTask<IReadOnlyList<T>> ListAsync( string? search, IReadOnlyList<string> searchFields, int skip, int take, CancellationToken ct); // after ValueTask<IReadOnlyList<T>> ListAsync( string? search, IReadOnlyList<string> searchFields, ScaffoldSortSpec? sort, IReadOnlyList<string> sortFields, ScaffoldCursor? after, int take, CancellationToken ct); CountAsync, GetAsync, CreateAsync, UpdateAsync and DeleteAsync are unchanged. What a consumer does Take the new parameters. sort is a property name the generated endpoint has already checked against the model's [ScaffoldSort] set, so a store does not re-validate it; a name it does not recognise falls back to the key order rather than throwing, because the only way that happens is a hand-written caller. sortFields is that allow-list, for a store that wants it. Page from after instead of skip. after is null for the first page. Otherwise it carries the boundary row's sort value (as text, or null for SQL NULL), its key, and whether the reader is travelling backwards. A backward page returns the rows before the boundary, still in display order — mirror the comparison and the ORDER BY, then reverse the rows. End every order with the key. The framework cannot enforce this and every guarantee it makes depends on it: a page boundary inside a block of equal sort values has no single answer without a tiebreaker, and the rows there repeat. Fix where NULLs go on a nullable sorted column, in the query rather than by default. (author, id) > (@a, @id) evaluates to NULL — not true — for an author-less row, so WHERE drops it and every page after the first silently omits every one of them. The reference app's db/sample/queries/Articles/ListByAuthorAsc.sql carries the widened predicate and the whole explanation. This is invisible on a table small enough to fit one page. Mark sortable columns. A column with no [ScaffoldSort] renders as an inert heading, which is how a reader tells what can order the list. The key is always sortable. A store with no ordering to give can satisfy the seam by ignoring sort and paging on the key alone; it will be correct, just not sortable. Callers of ListAsync that are not stores — anything that lists rows for its own purposes — pass (null, [], null, [], null, take, ct). 2026-08-31 — the identity-provider sign-in library removed Seam: the identity-provider-specific sign-in library (libs/nativeweb.signin.<provider>) and its Add<Provider>(...) builder on top of AddNativeWebSignIn. Commit: f743436. Decision: 007. Affects: anything registering that provider through NativeWeb. libs/nativeweb.signin — the generic OIDC/OAuth2 client, its session handler, bearer introspection filter and store seams — stays and is unchanged. What went is the provider-specific client, DTOs and registration built on top of it. A consumer configures the same flow through the generic library, supplying the provider's endpoints from configuration instead of getting them from a compiled-in provider class. This is the scope boundary the repository holds: Django ships contrib.auth and leaves the OAuth2/OIDC server to a third-party package. A sign-in client is framework; a named provider's integration is a product's. 2026-08-31 — libs/nativeweb.auth and libs/nativeweb.data removed Commit: f743436. Decision: 007. Affects: anything referencing TokenIssuer, SilentConsent, RealmResolver, SsoModes, WebAuthnPolicy, ClientAuthenticator, or the generated accessors over the identity schema. Removed together with apps/web (the identity provider), its db/ migrations of users, realms and refresh tokens, and seven test projects. None of it is ported and there is no replacement here. The identity provider is a separate product; decision 007 records why. The pieces of that tree that are framework stayed: password and token hashing, opaque token generation, bot traps and the data protector are in libs/nativeweb.security. A consumer that needs an identity provider uses one. A consumer that needs users and passwords builds them on the primitives, which is the contrib.auth side of the line."},{"u":"02-reference-configuration.html","t":"Configuration reference","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"the-section-prefix","t":"The section prefix"},{"i":"framework-settings","t":"Framework settings"},{"i":"outside-the-section","t":"Outside the section"},{"i":"three-that-stop-the-process","t":"Three that stop the process"},{"i":"the-reference-applications-own-key","t":"The reference application's own key"},{"i":"sign-in-settings","t":"Sign-in settings"},{"i":"what-a-provisioned-host-holds","t":"What a provisioned host holds"},{"i":"related","t":"Related"}],"b":"Configuration reference Every setting the framework reads, what it defaults to, and what a wrong or absent value costs. This page is enforced. tests/nativeweb.repo.tests.unit/DocsClaimsTests.cs extracts the key names from the two binders below at run time and fails when this file names a key the code does not read, or omits one it does. The section prefix NativeWeb reads nothing from a fixed key path. Every setting lives under a section the product chooses, passed to NativeWebOptions.FromConfiguration(builder.Configuration, \"<Name>\"). The reference application uses Sample (apps/sample/Program.cs); a product stamped from skeleton/ uses its own name. Keys below are written section-relative — read {Section} as whatever your composition root passed. Source When Notes appsettings.json always Committed. Never a secret User secrets Development only CreateSlimBuilder does not load them; add them explicitly under IsDevelopment() Environment variables always The deployment path Environment-variable spelling replaces : with __. Sample:Db:MaxAutoPrepare is Sample__Db__MaxAutoPrepare. Framework settings Bound by NativeWebOptions.TryFromConfiguration in libs/nativeweb.web/NativeWebOptions.cs. The On a bad value column is the one to read: three of these stop the process at startup with the key named on stderr, and the rest fall back silently. Key Default On a bad value {Section}:Db:ConnectionString none Null. The app answers /healthz but reports not-ready {Section}:Db:CommandTimeoutSeconds driver default Ignored unless a positive integer {Section}:Db:MaxAutoPrepare 20 Falls back to 20 {Section}:ForwardedHeaders:KnownProxies empty Unparseable entries are skipped, not fatal — a typo costs a degraded rate-limit partition, not an outage. Required behind a proxy: deploy.sh refuses to promote a release without it, because unset means ASP.NET Core trusts loopback only, RemoteIpAddress is the proxy hop, and every client shares one rate-limit partition with nothing logged {Section}:ForwardedHeaders:ForwardLimit 1 Fails startup unless a positive whole number. The number of proxy hops in front of the app, and separate from KnownProxies: that decides which hops may be unwound, this caps how many are, so a chain longer than the cap stops early however well-trusted its hops are. provision.sh --edge builds two hops and seeds 2. Raising it opens no spoofing surface — every step is still gated by the trust check {Section}:Telemetry:Enabled false Fails startup. \"1\", \"yes\" and \"Enabled\" are all things an operator writes meaning on, and every one of them would read as off under an equality check {Section}:DataProtection:KeyDirectory a directory under the process's home A configured value is taken as given and never probed — an operator's explicit statement, checked instead by deploy.sh's preflight against ReadWritePaths. Only the fallback is proved, and fails startup when there is no home to fall back to {Section}:Uploads:Directory an uploads directory beside the data-protection key ring, or under it when that has no parent Read as configuration, and created and probed once at startup, not by the binder: NativeWebOptions.TryPrepareUploadDirectory makes it if it is absent and fails startup when it exists and this process cannot write to it. Unwritable has to be fatal rather than deferred, because otherwise every upload fails after the deploy that made it read-only with nothing reporting it {Section}:BotTraps:MinAgeSeconds unset, and the trap still runs: BotTrapOptions.MinAge is 3 s and the binder overrides it only when the key is set Fails startup unless a non-negative number {Section}:RateLimit:General:PermitLimit 120 Falls back to 120; 0 and negatives take the default rather than opening or closing the policy {Section}:RateLimit:CspReport:PermitLimit 10 Falls back to 10 {Section}:Readiness:CacheSeconds 1 Clamped to a 30-second maximum {Section}:SecurityContact unset, serves no /security.txt Fails startup unless a bare email address or a mailto:, https: or tel: URI on one line Outside the section Key Default Notes ConnectionStrings:{Section} none What apps/sample reads, in preference to {Section}:Db:ConnectionString. Fill the one your binary uses OTEL_SERVICE_NAME the application name At the configuration root, not under the section: it is the standard environment variable OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT none Read by the OpenTelemetry exporter itself. deploy.sh refuses to promote when telemetry is on and this is unset Three that stop the process Telemetry:Enabled, DataProtection:KeyDirectory and SecurityContact fail startup rather than defaulting, and each for the same reason: the silent version of the failure is invisible. Telemetry an operator believes is exporting and is not; a key ring that cannot persist, so every restart remints the antiforgery keys and every form POST answers 400 after a deploy while /readyz stays green; a /security.txt nobody can act on. KeyDirectory is the partial case: what stops the process is having no usable fallback, not a configured directory that turns out to be unwritable, which the deploy preflight catches rather than startup. The exit is code 78 with the key named on stderr — never a modal dialog, which under NativeAOT on Windows a supervisor reads as a hung service. The reference application's own key apps/sample reads one setting the framework does not, under its own Sample section. It is the sample's, not a framework key: a single shared passphrase in front of one product's admin is that product's decision, and a product stamped from skeleton/ signs in through its OAuth provider instead. Key Default On a missing value Sample:Admin:Passphrase none Production fails startup with the key named, exit 78. Any other environment but Development serves the form at /signin and refuses every attempt. Development ignores it: there /signin signs the visitor in without a form On a deployed host it lives in /etc/nativeweb/secrets.env as Sample__Admin__Passphrase, which provision.sh seeds blank and deploy.sh refuses to promote without, a quoted empty value included. The provisioner writes only a secrets.env that does not exist yet, so a host provisioned before the key existed gets the line added by hand. The comparison is constant-time over a digest of each side, the POST is rate-limited to 10 attempts per client address per minute, and a refusal is one fixed page whatever was typed. Sign-in settings Bound by SignInConfiguration in libs/nativeweb.signin/SignInConfiguration.cs, under a section the product names when it calls AddSignIn. See libs/nativeweb.signin/README.md for what the library does with them. Key Default AuthorizationEndpoint, TokenEndpoint, UserInfoEndpoint, IntrospectionEndpoint empty ClientId, ClientSecret, RedirectUri empty ClientAuthentication ClientSecretBasic Scopes the library's default set, replaced wholesale when configured, never merged CookieName __Host-signin LoginPath /signin CallbackPath /signin/callback LogoutPath /signout SignedOutPath, FailurePath, DefaultReturnPath / FlowLifetimeSeconds 15 minutes SessionLifetimeSeconds 14 days RefreshSkewSeconds 2 minutes IntrospectionCacheLifetimeSeconds 30 seconds RetainTokens true StoreEncryptionKey none. Base64, because that is what a secret manager hands back and the only encoding that survives an environment variable without a length surprise. It must decode to exactly StoreDataProtector.KeyBytes, and a single repeated byte is refused outright — new byte[32] is what a placeholder and a copied example both look like, and it boots a host whose stored provider tokens anyone can decrypt. Generate one with RandomNumberGenerator.GetBytes(32) AllowEphemeralStoreEncryptionKey false What a provisioned host holds scripts/deploy/provision.sh seeds five files under /etc/nativeweb/. It writes them empty where a value is a secret: a script that generates a production credential is a script that has seen it. File Mode Holds app.env 0644 root:<group> ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT, the forwarded-headers stanza, DataProtection:KeyDirectory, Telemetry:Enabled secrets.env 0640 root:<group> The connection string, in both spellings — fill the one your binary reads and delete the other blue.env, green.env 0644 root:<group> ASPNETCORE_URLS for each colour, ports 5011 and 5012 backup.env 0640 root:root Read by scripts/deploy/backup.sh and scripts/deploy/restore.sh, not by the application DataProtection:KeyDirectory must stay inside /var/lib/nativeweb: ReadWritePaths in the systemd unit names that directory and ProtectSystem=strict makes the rest read-only. Related deployment.md — the runbook these files serve. libs/nativeweb.web/README.md — the pipeline that reads the framework settings. libs/nativeweb.signin/README.md — the relying party that reads the sign-in settings."},{"u":"02-reference-continuous-audit.html","t":"Continuous audit","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"what-it-does-once-a-night","t":"What it does, once a night"},{"i":"why-it-cannot-damage-anything","t":"Why it cannot damage anything"},{"i":"provisioning-once","t":"Provisioning, once"},{"i":"reading-a-digest","t":"Reading a digest"},{"i":"triaging","t":"Triaging"},{"i":"stopping-it","t":"Stopping it"},{"i":"where-things-live","t":"Where things live"},{"i":"the-checks","t":"The checks"},{"i":"running-it-by-hand","t":"Running it by hand"},{"i":"cost","t":"Cost"}],"b":"Continuous audit A nightly, read-only pass over one slice of this repository, run on a machine that is not the workstation, producing findings a session triages by hand. Spec docs/specs/013-continuous-audit/. You should be able to provision it, read a digest, act on a finding and stop the schedule without opening a .ts file. That is what this page is for. What it does, once a night The workstation has pushed a mirror of the repository to the audit host. At 03:07 (the hour is --hour, default 3) the audit host clones that mirror into a temporary directory. It picks the least recently audited slice — a directory that directly contains source — and reads only that. Slices never audited come first. It runs six mechanical checks over the slice, then, if the slice is under 120 KB and a GLM key is present, one bounded reading pass. Every candidate finding is put to a second model prompted to refute it. Refuted findings are dropped and counted; findings that cannot be evaluated are admitted as unverified. What survives is redacted, then appended to the ledger. The clone is deleted. A run only marks the slice as read when it read it through. A slice over the byte bound, or a reading pass the model cut short at its token limit, leaves the slice where it was in the rotation, so it comes round again rather than being recorded as covered. It writes nothing to this repository, ever. There is no flag that makes it. Why it cannot damage anything Guarantee How, structurally The audit cannot modify the repository It works on a throwaway clone in a temp directory, deleted whether the run succeeds or throws The audit cannot reach origin The audit host holds no credential for it; the repository reaches the host by push, not pull A secret in the source cannot leak into a report 13 redaction rules run before anything is written, and every written artefact is re-scanned afterwards; a non-zero corpusLeaks is a defect in the audit itself A finding you dismissed cannot come back rejected is an absorbing state, on write and on read A quiet week cannot look like a clean repository A day with no run record is reported as a gap, above the findings — and the gap window runs to today, so a scheduler that died a month ago shows a month of gaps rather than an unbroken history An unreachable audit host cannot look like a clean repository The digest refuses to render (exit 4) rather than reporting an empty ledger it could not read Provisioning, once Run on the audit host, by you. It installs software and loads a scheduler, which is not an agent's call. scp scripts/audit/provision-audit-host.sh audit-host.local:~/ ssh audit-host.local ./provision-audit-host.sh # dry run: prints every step, changes nothing ./provision-audit-host.sh --execute # installs Deno to ~/.deno, creates ~/audit, loads launchd It refuses with exit 3 if the host holds a production credential — ~/.aws/credentials, ~/.pgpass, a gh token and so on — before installing anything, so a refusal leaves the machine as it found it. --accept-credential-risk proceeds anyway and says so. The one secret the host needs is the GLM key in ~/.zai. Without it the reading pass is skipped and the mechanical checks still run. Then, from the workstation: scripts/audit/sync-to-audit-host.sh # dry run: what it would mirror scripts/audit/sync-to-audit-host.sh --execute # mirror every branch to the audit host ssh audit-host.local 'git clone -q ~/audit/mirror/nativeweb.git ~/audit/nativeweb' Re-run sync-to-audit-host.sh --execute whenever you want the audit to see newer work. It pushes to the audit host and never to origin; origin is yours. Reading a digest From the workstation. This reads over ssh and writes nothing on the audit host. deno run -A tools/audit/digest.ts --ssh audit-host.local deno run -A tools/audit/digest.ts --ssh audit-host.local --json > tmp/digest.json If the host cannot be reached it exits 4 and prints why, rather than rendering an empty digest — \"there are no findings\" and \"the store could not be read\" are not the same statement. The report opens with health, deliberately: if the scheduler is dead, nothing below that line means what it appears to mean. Then coverage, then untriaged findings with verified ones first, each carrying the file, the line, what, why, and a command to confirm it. Running it twice without triaging shows the same list. That is the intended behaviour — the list is \"everything not yet decided\", not \"everything since yesterday\". Triaging A finding is worth acting on, or it is not, and either way that is a decision someone makes. # Dismiss it. Absorbing: it will never appear in a digest again. deno run -A tools/audit/triage.ts --ssh audit-host.local \\ --id a1b2c3d4e5f6 --state rejected --reason \"cross-repo note, not this repository\" # Agree to act on it. Requires a recorded approver. deno run -A tools/audit/triage.ts --ssh audit-host.local \\ --id a1b2c3d4e5f6 --state accepted --reason \"broken cross-reference\" --approved-by <name> # Record that it is done. deno run -A tools/audit/triage.ts --ssh audit-host.local \\ --id a1b2c3d4e5f6 --state fixed --reason \"landed in <commit>\" --id takes any unambiguous prefix of at least six characters. An ambiguous one is an error, not a guess. On --approved-by. It is a record, not an enforcement boundary: anything with a shell can type a flag. What holds is structural — nothing in the audit path calls triage.ts. The audit writes findings and never decides about them. --approved-by exists so the ledger says who agreed and when, rather than the tooling assuming you did. Rejecting needs no approver, because declining to act is reversible with one more line while accepting is what leads to a branch and a diff. Stopping it ssh audit-host.local \"launchctl bootout gui/\\$(id -u)/com.extrode.nativeweb.audit\" The ledger, coverage and run records stay under ~/audit. Re-run the provisioner to start again. To pause for one night, do nothing: the digest will report the gap, which is the point. Where things live What Where The ledger of record audit-host:~/audit/ledger/nativeweb.jsonl — append-only JSONL Slice coverage audit-host:~/audit/ledger/nativeweb-coverage.json One record per run audit-host:~/audit/runs/<runId>.json launchd stdout/stderr audit-host:~/audit/runs/launchd.{out,err}.log The repository mirror audit-host:~/audit/mirror/nativeweb.git The tooling checkout audit-host:~/audit/nativeweb A finding's identity is a hash of its path, rule and normalised excerpt — not its line number, so editing the code above a defect does not resurrect it as a new finding. The checks Rule Fires when stale-todo A TODO is both old (by blame) and unattributed — no owner, issue, spec, requirement, date or URL doc-names-missing-file A Markdown document names a repository path that no tracked file matches, under any reading assertionless-test An xUnit [Fact]/[Theory] or Deno.test body contains no assertion spec-requirement-without-criterion A spec requirement has no matching acceptance criterion spec-done-with-open-task A spec is marked done with unticked tasks spec-done-with-unmet-requirement A spec is marked done while its own text says a requirement is unmet Plus one reading pass per run, over the selected slice, whose findings go through the same refutation and redaction as the mechanical ones. Running it by hand cd tools/audit deno test -A -q # the suite deno run -A run.ts --source ../.. --store ../../tmp/audit-smoke --no-model --slice docs/02-reference --no-model runs the mechanical checks only, makes no network call, and costs nothing. Cost The reading pass uses glm-5.3 over the z.ai Anthropic-compatible endpoint, which draws z.ai plan credits. Each run records its token counts, and the digest shows the trend, so drift is visible before it is a bill. No dollar figure is claimed unless you supply a price with --cost-in and --cost-out — a fabricated number would be believed."},{"u":"02-reference-deployment.html","t":"Deployment runbook","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"shape-of-it","t":"Shape of it"},{"i":"decisions-on-record","t":"Decisions on record"},{"i":"provisioning-a-host","t":"Provisioning a host"},{"i":"required-configuration","t":"Required configuration"},{"i":"releasing","t":"Releasing"},{"i":"migrations-run-against-the-previous-release","t":"Migrations run against the previous release"},{"i":"rolling-back","t":"Rolling back"},{"i":"what-is-deployed-right-now","t":"What is deployed right now"},{"i":"the-documentation-site-at-docs","t":"The documentation site, at /docs"},{"i":"backups","t":"Backups"},{"i":"knowing-a-backup-failed","t":"Knowing a backup failed"},{"i":"restoring","t":"Restoring"},{"i":"disaster-recovery","t":"Disaster recovery"},{"i":"known-gaps","t":"Known gaps"}],"b":"Deployment runbook Spec: ../specs/011-production-deployment/011-spec.md (the R-numbers below are its requirements) Scripts: ../../scripts/deploy/ The one document for provisioning, releasing, rolling back, backing up and recovering a NativeWeb production host. Everything here is a command to run or a decision with a stated default. build.sh publishes apps/sample by default and takes PROJECT=<csproj> for a stamped product's own app. It installs the executable under the fixed name nativeweb, so nothing downstream (deploy.sh, nativeweb@.service, Caddy) names the project. Releasing, restoring and rebuilding a guest from backup have all been run on a real host. What has never been rehearsed is losing the machine. The backup bucket was an S3-compatible service on the physical host being backed up, so losing that machine loses the guest, the replacement and the bucket together. R14 and R17b are unmet. R17b needs the backup to be readable after the host is gone, which takes both the bytes and the age key; the key is held off-host and the bytes are not. R14 is about location alone. R15 is unmet too: BACKUP_HEARTBEAT_URL is unset, so a backup that stops running goes unnoticed. Shape of it One Linux host. Caddy terminates TLS on 443 and proxies to whichever release colour is live; two systemd instances of nativeweb@.service run on loopback ports 5011 (blue) and 5012 (green); PostgreSQL runs on the same box, bound to localhost. A deploy stages into the idle colour, migrates, proves readiness, and then reloads Caddy — so the live colour serves continuously and a failed release never reaches the public. Path What /opt/nativeweb/releases/<sha>/ Unpacked releases, root-owned /opt/nativeweb/{blue,green} Symlinks into a release /opt/nativeweb/scripts/ backup.sh, restore.sh, rollback.sh on the host /etc/nativeweb/app.env Non-secret settings, 0644 root:nativeweb /etc/nativeweb/secrets.env Secrets, 0640 root:nativeweb /etc/nativeweb/{blue,green}.env The colour → port mapping /etc/nativeweb/backup.env Backup destination and credentials, 0640 root:root /etc/caddy/upstream.caddy Generated. One line. Which colour is live /var/lib/nativeweb/keys Data-protection keys — must survive a release swap /var/lib/nativeweb/deployments.log What was promoted, when, and whether it migrated Decisions on record Both are choices rather than defaults. Distribution: Debian stable, x86-64. provision.sh targets apt and systemd; Ubuntu LTS works unchanged. Tested on Debian 12 (bookworm), x86-64, a VM guest. unattended-upgrades is on, security pocket only, and does NOT reboot. There is one host and no failover, so an unattended 03:00 reboot is an unannounced outage. Kernel and libc updates therefore wait for an operator: check /var/run/reboot-required on a monthly pass and reboot deliberately. Provisioning a host Prerequisites: a Debian host, root over SSH, and a DNS A/AAAA record pointing at it. Caddy cannot get a certificate before DNS resolves. git clone <this repo> && cd nativeweb sudo scripts/deploy/provision.sh Idempotent — safe to re-run after editing a unit file. Two things it deliberately will not do on a re-run: overwrite an /etc/nftables.conf an operator has edited (the ruleset opens with flush ruleset, which would drop every other table on the host — it prints what to merge by hand instead), and restart PostgreSQL when listen_addresses is already local by any spelling. It installs packages, creates the nativeweb system account with no interactive shell, lays out the directories, confines PostgreSQL to localhost, installs the units, the nftables ruleset and the Caddy config, bounds journal retention, and enables the backup timer. It writes no secrets; a script that generates production keys is a script that has seen them. The firewall guard accepts an /etc/nftables.conf that is absent, that carries our marker, or that is Debian's packaged conffile unmodified (dpkg --verify reports it clean). Debian's nftables package ships that file, so a guard that accepted only the first two would refuse every first provision and leave the host with no firewall. Then, in order: Every key below is spelled <Prefix>__…, where <Prefix> is the configuration section the deployed binary reads — Sample for apps/sample, your product's own name for anything stamped from skeleton/. Pass it to provision.sh --prefix and build.sh stamps the matching config_prefix into the artifact, which is where deploy.sh reads it from. Fill in /etc/nativeweb/secrets.env. The connection string, and nothing else. Two spellings are seeded and one of them applies: ConnectionStrings__<Prefix> for apps/sample, <Prefix>__Db__ConnectionString for a stamped product. Delete the other. Fill in the blanks in /etc/nativeweb/app.env. Checklist below. Set NATIVEWEB_HOST and NATIVEWEB_ACME_EMAIL in /etc/default/caddy, then systemctl restart caddy. deploy.sh refuses to promote while any of the four keys below is blank. An EnvironmentFile line with nothing after the = sets the variable to the empty string, not to absent, so a code fallback does not fire. Generate the backup key. sudo age-keygen -o /etc/nativeweb/backup-age.key sudo chmod 0600 /etc/nativeweb/backup-age.key Store the private half somewhere that is neither this host nor the backup bucket. This is the single point of failure in the whole design: lose this key and every object in the bucket is unreadable noise, and no amount of working backups helps. A password manager, a second machine, a printed copy in a safe — anywhere, as long as losing the host does not lose the key. Keep the private half in the operator's own store, and do not name that store in this file: a runbook that names the vault holding the key is half of the compromise. A key that was printed into a terminal session exists in whatever transcript, scrollback or shell history that session left behind; do not carry such a key to production. Rotating means age-keygen afresh, a new BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT, and keeping the old private key for as long as objects encrypted to the old recipient are inside the retention window. Put the public half in BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT in /etc/nativeweb/backup.env, along with the bucket, endpoint and access keys. provision.sh also adds a pg_ident map so that root — and only root, plus each account for its own role — can reach the nativeweb database over the unix socket as either nativeweb or postgres. That is how deploy.sh, backup.sh and restore.sh authenticate with no password in any file. Migrations need both: they create objects (and whoever creates them owns them, since nothing in the migrations issues a GRANT), and an extensions migration needs a superuser. Apply migrations. The first deploy does this. A migration directory that carries an NNNN_extensions.sql needs it applied by a superuser; see db/migrate.sh. Required configuration Four keys, all enforced by deploy.sh's preflight, all spelled with the prefix stamped into the artifact. Every check fails when the app would misbehave and passes when it would not — a preflight that passes while the thing it prevents is happening is worse than none, because it is also an assurance. Key Why <Prefix>__ForwardedHeaders__KnownProxies Unset behind Caddy means RemoteIpAddress is the proxy hop and every client in the world shares one rate-limit partition. Nothing errors and nothing logs it ConnectionStrings__<Prefix> or <Prefix>__Db__ConnectionString Absent, apps/sample serves its in-memory demo store with a green /healthz, and a stamped product times out the /readyz poll with no named cause <Prefix>__DataProtection__KeyDirectory Must be under /var/lib/nativeweb. That is the only path ReadWritePaths names, and ProtectSystem=strict makes the rest read-only. Unset, the key ring lands under a read-only working directory and cannot persist: every restart remints the antiforgery keys and every form POST answers 400 after each deploy, with /readyz green. The app now refuses to start rather than doing that silently OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT, when <Prefix>__Telemetry__Enabled=true Telemetry is opt-in: the framework defaults Enabled to false. The preflight fails only when it is on with nowhere to send Releasing Build on Linux. NativeAOT does not cross-compile, so a linux-x64 publish must happen on Linux. From the repository root (under WSL on Windows): scripts/deploy/build.sh # refuses a dirty tree; --force overrides scripts/deploy/deploy.sh --artifact dist/nativeweb-<sha>.tar.zst --host <host> deploy.sh copies itself along with the artifact and re-executes on the host under sudo, so the deploy procedure and the release it deploys are always the same version. What it does, in order — every step before the last leaves production untouched: Refuses if KnownProxies is unset or telemetry has no destination. Reads which colour is live from /etc/caddy/upstream.caddy; picks the other. Exits 0 unchanged if that release is already live. Unpacks into /opt/nativeweb/releases/<sha> and points the idle symlink at it. Runs db/migrate.sh. Aborts on failure, live colour still serving. Starts the idle colour, polls its own /readyz. Abandons on timeout, stops the idle unit, prints the last 40 journal lines, live colour still serving. Rewrites upstream.caddy and systemctl reload caddy — graceful, so in-flight requests finish on the old colour. Records the promotion and prunes old release directories. A reference run on a cold host (nothing serving, so step 3 had no live colour to compare against and step 8 had nothing to prune): Measure Value deploy.sh, artifact on the host to promoted 1.774 s /readyz through the public origin 200, body ready, 0.022 s /healthz 200, 0.023 s The timings include Caddy and TLS but not a WAN round trip. Build time is not recorded: one measurement on one build box is not a figure this runbook should quote. Exit 78 in step 6's log is EX_CONFIG: a configuration error, not a crash. The unit will not restart it (RestartPreventExitStatus=78), because the config will not have changed by the time it comes back. Migrations run against the previous release Step 5 happens while the old binary is still serving. Two consequences: Expand now, contract later. A migration that drops or renames something the running version reads takes production down between steps 5 and 7. Additive changes only in the release that introduces them; remove the old column in a later release. Split long migrations. db/migrate.sh applies each file in one transaction, so every lock the file takes is held until its last statement commits. Against an idle database that is free; against a live one it blocks readers and writers for the file's duration. See migration-lock-review.md. Rolling back sudo /opt/nativeweb/scripts/rollback.sh # dry run sudo /opt/nativeweb/scripts/rollback.sh --execute A Caddy reload and nothing else — the previous colour is still running, which is why deploy.sh leaves it up. It refuses a release that applied migrations, naming it, because nothing records whether those were an expand or a contract and this script must not guess. For a contracting release the way back is a forward release that reverses it. What is deployed right now curl -s https://<host>/version # from inside the running process cat /opt/nativeweb/blue/RELEASE # or green — works with nothing running tail /var/lib/nativeweb/deployments.log The documentation site, at /docs scripts/build-docs.sh renders docs/ into the committed, versioned docs-site/, and from there it travels in the artifact exactly as db/ does. Nothing extra to run at release time: Where What docs-site/ in the repo the tracked site, one frozen directory per release plus unversioned/ docs_dir= in RELEASE docs when this artifact carries a site, empty when it does not /opt/nativeweb/docs a symlink to the live colour's docs/, repointed at promotion and at rollback handle_path /docs/* in the Caddyfile serves it; redir /docs /docs/ 308 in front, because the index's links are relative Absent docs are not an error. A product that has never run build-docs.sh builds and deploys normally, and /docs is a 404 until it does. A DOCS_DIR that exists without a versions.json is an error: that is either the wrong directory or a half-finished build, and shipping it would put a directory listing under the product's own hostname. The docs follow the release, so a rollback rolls them back too — which matters less than it sounds, because the site is versioned: every release's documentation stays in the tree whichever one is serving. A guest behind an edge proxy needs a different Caddyfile from the tracked one: TLS terminates at the edge, so it is auto_https off on :80 with trusted_proxies. The /docs block above is the only part the two share verbatim. Backups Nightly at 03:17 UTC (plus up to 15 minutes of jitter), Persistent=true so a host that was off catches up rather than skipping a day silently. pg_dump -Fc piped straight into age — the plaintext dump never touches disk — then uploaded to the configured S3-compatible endpoint as <host>/<UTC timestamp>.dump.age, and objects past the retention period (default 30 days) are deleted. sudo /opt/nativeweb/scripts/backup.sh # dry run: what it would do sudo /opt/nativeweb/scripts/backup.sh --execute systemctl list-timers nativeweb-backup.timer Accepted limit: up to 24 hours of data can be lost. Nightly granularity is the decided recovery point; there is no continuous archiving. On record as a decision, not an oversight. Knowing a backup failed Two mechanisms, covering two different failures: A backup that runs and fails triggers nativeweb-backup-failed.service, which logs the last 40 lines and pings the heartbeat URL's /fail endpoint. A backup that never runs at all is invisible to systemd — nothing failed, because nothing happened. Only an external dead-man's-switch catches it: backup.sh pings BACKUP_HEARTBEAT_URL on success, and the watcher alerts when the ping does not arrive. BACKUP_HEARTBEAT_URL is unset, so R15 is not met by this script. A backup that silently stops running will not be noticed. Point it at an external check (a dead-man's-switch service) to close it. backup.sh says so in its own output on every run rather than letting the gap pass quietly. Restoring Drill against a scratch database. Never against production by accident — the script requires --overwrite-production on top of --execute for that. sudo /opt/nativeweb/scripts/restore.sh --target nativeweb_drill # dry run sudo /opt/nativeweb/scripts/restore.sh --target nativeweb_drill --execute pg_restore exiting 0 means the rows loaded, not that the application works against them. The drill is not finished until you have pointed a NativeWeb instance at the restored database and served a row that existed when the backup was taken, while a row written after it is absent. apps/sample keeps no accounts in the database -- its only sign-in is a passphrase read from secrets.env -- so \"signed in as an account that existed when the backup was taken\" would pass against an empty database and against no restore at all. Writing a marker row after the backup makes the check falsifiable: the restored database must not have it. Measure Value Time to download and restore 1 s (10,359-byte dump, 8 MB database) Time to a verified serve against restored data 1 s Those figures come from a drill that wrote one marker article before the backup and one after it. The restored database held the first and not the second, and the release served the first 200 and the second 404 against it. The dump is small because the database was nearly empty; the figures are a floor, not a capacity measurement. Disaster recovery Losing the host is an outage, not a failover — one host, by decision. This is the whole way back, from nothing but this repository and the backup bucket. Provision a new host: Provisioning a host, above. Restore the age private key from the operator's off-host store. Without it, stop: the bucket is noise. Install age and the AWS CLI if the image does not carry them, and make sure the backup bucket accepts connections from the new host's address. Fill BACKUP_S3_BUCKET, BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT, AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID and AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY in the new host's /etc/nativeweb/backup.env. provision.sh seeds them empty and restore.sh dies on the first of them. Carry secrets.env and app.env across from wherever they are kept. Set the app role's password to match that secrets.env: sudo -u postgres psql -c \"alter role nativeweb with password '...'\". pg_dump -Fc dumps one database, not roles, so the restore does not bring it. restore.sh --target nativeweb --from-host <old-host> --overwrite-production --execute. --from-host is not optional here if the new machine has a different name. backup.sh writes under the writing host's short name, and restore.sh derives the same prefix from the host it is running on — so a rebuilt machine reports \"no backups found\" against a bucket that is full of them. Omit the flag only when the replacement carries the old hostname. Build and deploy the last known-good release: Releasing, above. Point DNS at the new host and wait for Caddy to obtain a certificate. Measure Value Total time, bare host to serving 345 s (5 m 45 s) provision.sh --edge <edge-ip> 26 s Measured rebuilding a guest on the same physical host: guest created, provision.sh, age key installed, restore.sh --target nativeweb --from-host <old-host> --overwrite-production --execute, deploy.sh --incus, then /healthz 200, /readyz 200 and the pre-backup article served. Four caveats on that number: The artifact was not rebuilt. The running release was repackaged with tar --zstd, the same shape build.sh emits. A real rebuild adds however long a publish takes to the 345 s. The bucket was an S3-compatible service on the same physical host. Losing that host loses the guest, the replacement guest and the bucket together, so this exercises R17a and leaves R17b and R14 unmet. The age key is held off-host, which removes one of R17b's two blockers (readable needs the key and the bytes) and neither of R14's, which is about location alone. Four preconditions were found by running it: the DR host needs the bucket, endpoint and both AWS keys in its own backup.env (provision.sh seeds them empty and restore.sh dies on the first), age and the AWS CLI must be installed, the object storage must allow the new host's address, and the app role's password must be set to match the carried secrets.env, because pg_dump -Fc dumps one database, not roles. They are steps 3 to 6. provision.sh must run inside the guest. On a shared physical host it would overwrite /etc/caddy/Caddyfile, which serves every site on the machine, and its nftables ruleset opens with flush ruleset. Known gaps Gap Status No backup is offsite R14 and R17b unmet. Every backup drilled so far lived on the physical host being backed up. Closed when a backup held on separate storage has been restored from MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes verified on WSL2, not on the target host Closed with a caveat; see below BACKUP_HEARTBEAT_URL unset R15 unmet until an operator sets it, for any deployment running backup.sh On MemoryDenyWriteExecute. The unit sets MemoryDenyWriteExecute=yes. A linux-x64 publish was checked under that filter on Debian 12 under WSL2: it started, logged \"Now listening\", and answered /healthz 200 and /readyz 503 (the no-database state), the same as with the filter off. The lazy dlopen of libsodium.so behind Argon2id never runs at startup, so it was probed separately under the same filter: dlopen succeeded and sodium_init() returned 0. What is not proven is a --system unit on Debian bare metal. If a first boot there dies without ever logging, this line is the first thing to comment out."},{"u":"02-reference-migration-lock-review.html","t":"Migration lock duration: review before pointing migrate.sh at a live database","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"the-rule-of-thumb","t":"The rule of thumb"},{"i":"files-that-need-care","t":"Files that need care"},{"i":"what-to-do-about-it","t":"What to do about it"},{"i":"expand-now-contract-later","t":"Expand now, contract later"}],"b":"Migration lock duration: review before pointing migrate.sh at a live database db/migrate.sh wraps each migration file in one transaction. That is right for atomicity and costs lock duration: every lock a file takes is held until its last statement commits, where autocommit would release them statement by statement. Against a freshly provisioned database that is free. Against a database serving traffic it blocks readers and writers for the length of the file. The migrations in db/sample/ run against a database being built, so nothing there needs changing. This is the checklist for writing migrations that will run against a live database, such as a stamped product's own db/migrations/. The rule of thumb Statement Lock Duration ADD COLUMN with a constant default ACCESS EXCLUSIVE Instant since PG 11 — no rewrite ALTER COLUMN … TYPE ACCESS EXCLUSIVE Full table rewrite SET NOT NULL ACCESS EXCLUSIVE Full table scan ADD CONSTRAINT … FOREIGN KEY SHARE ROW EXCLUSIVE on both tables Full validation scan unless NOT VALID CREATE INDEX blocks writes Full build CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY does not block writes Two scans, cannot be in a transaction Unqualified UPDATE row locks, whole table Proportional to rows Files that need care Only wrapped files are affected. A file carrying -- @no-transaction is applied statement by statement, so every CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY migration must carry the marker. Watch for: Several tables altered in one file. Each ALTER TABLE holds its lock until the file commits, so the tables are locked together, not one after another. ADD COLUMN then a whole-table UPDATE then SET NOT NULL then ADD CONSTRAINT ... FOREIGN KEY then CREATE INDEX on one table, repeated across tables. That sequence is the worst shape. ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE, which rewrites the table under ACCESS EXCLUSIVE. Non-concurrent CREATE INDEX on a table that takes writes. What to do about it In order of preference: Provision fresh instead. If the cutover creates a new database and imports data, none of this applies. Split by table. One file per table, each with its own @no-transaction where it needs one. Each is still atomic in the sense that matters (a half-applied table is recoverable, a half-applied schema is not), and no two tables are locked simultaneously. ADD CONSTRAINT … NOT VALID, then VALIDATE CONSTRAINT separately. The second takes only SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE and does not block writes. Make the non-concurrent CREATE INDEX statements concurrent, which moves those files to @no-transaction. Set a lock_timeout for the run so a migration that cannot get its lock quickly fails instead of queuing behind — and ahead of — application traffic. Expand now, contract later deploy.sh runs migrations while the previous release is still serving, so a migration must not drop or rename anything the running version reads. See the runbook: deployment.md."},{"u":"02-reference-performance.html","t":"Performance log","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"measurements","t":"Measurements"},{"i":"bottlenecks-found-in-the-order-they-were-found","t":"Bottlenecks found, in the order they were found"},{"i":"1-a-chunked-response-on-every-page--35-of-throughput-fixed","t":"1. A chunked response, on every page — 35% of throughput (fixed)"},{"i":"2-the-driver-not-the-server-open-and-probably-not-worth-closing","t":"2. The driver, not the server (open, and probably not worth closing)"},{"i":"3-two-locale-lookups-per-page-open-unmeasured","t":"3. Two locale lookups per page (open, unmeasured)"},{"i":"4-response-compression--63-of-articles-throughput-fixed-by-moving-it","t":"4. Response compression — 63% of articles throughput (fixed, by moving it)"},{"i":"what-has-never-been-measured","t":"What has never been measured"}],"b":"Performance log Every load measurement taken on this repository, newest first, with what changed between one row and the next. tools/bench/README.md explains the harness; this file is the record it produces. Numbers here are machine-bound and comparable only to each other. All of them were taken on one workstation (16 logical cores, Windows 11), against apps/sample published Release with NativeAOT, over plain HTTP on loopback, 16 concurrent workers, three timed runs of 10 s averaged after a discarded warmup. tools/bench/baseline.json carries the machine fingerprint and the harness records rather than compares when it does not match. healthz is the control. It renders nothing, so it measures the driver, the socket and Kestrel's floor. A change that moves articles and healthz by the same proportion did not cost anything in the render path — it moved the whole machine. Only the ratio between them says anything about a page. Measurements Change articles req/s p50 p99 healthz req/s peak RSS articles/healthz Compression moved to Caddy — current baseline 16,528.8 0.93ms 2.40ms 23,183.5 54.0 MB 0.713 Framework web pipeline adopted, compressing in-process 6,440.5 2.30ms 6.52ms 19,539.8 53.5 MB 0.330 Review fixes (author match, Take echoed) 18,958.6 0.70ms 2.30ms 23,324.5 35.6 MB 0.813 Content-Length declared on template pages 19,051.8 0.69ms 2.30ms 22,655.4 35.4 MB 0.841 Rendering through compiled templates 12,124.2 1.18ms 3.76ms 22,523.7 38.7 MB 0.538 Design system + styled page 18,453.4 0.67ms 2.74ms 20,953.5 47.0 MB 0.881 Baseline, hand-written StringBuilder renderer 19,937.6 — — 23,068.6 45.0 MB 0.864 Zero errors in every run of every row. Where it stands. The top row is the current state and the committed baseline. A pipeline of ten middleware where there were none costs articles 12.8% (18,958.6 → 16,528.8) and healthz nothing measurable (23,324.5 → 23,183.5, inside the run-to-run spread). Peak RSS rose 35.6 → 54.0 MB, which is ten registrations of services that were absent before. The second row is what the same pipeline cost while it compressed in-process, and it is kept because the difference between the two rows is the whole of bottleneck 4: one middleware was worth 157% of this page's throughput. Between the third and fourth rows, two behavioural fixes, not a perf change: matching authors as well as titles adds one null-checked Contains per row over at most twenty rows, and echoing Take adds one integer to the render. Both are inside the noise, which the ratio moving 0.841 → 0.813 across adjacent runs of near-identical code is itself a measure of. Bottlenecks found, in the order they were found 1. A chunked response, on every page — 35% of throughput (fixed) TemplateResults.Html wrote into Response.BodyWriter and flushed once. That reads like the streaming path, and is not: the body was fully materialized before the flush either way, so nothing was ever being streamed. What the missing Content-Length did was make every page a chunked response. Three variants of the identical page separate the two candidate causes: variant req/s p50 straight to BodyWriter, no length 11,779 1.24ms buffered first, still no length 11,778 1.24ms buffered first, length declared 18,105 0.71ms The middle row is the one that matters. Buffering changes nothing, so the cost was never the many small GetSpan/Advance calls into Kestrel's pipe — the theory that looked most likely going in. It is the framing alone. Fixed in libs/nativeweb.render/TemplateResults.cs; guarded by TemplateHtmlResultTests, because no assertion about markup can tell a chunked page from a measured one. A count off the wire is worth recording alongside it: the chunked response carried two chunk headers, not one per write. Kestrel was already coalescing. The cost is the framing, not fragmentation. 2. The driver, not the server (open, and probably not worth closing) The harness is a Deno fetch loop, because no load tool is installed on this machine and Deno is the mandated runtime. A route that renders nothing tops out around 22.7k req/s, which is a statement about fetch and loopback rather than about Kestrel. Every number here is therefore a floor on what the server can do, and small absolute differences between adjacent rows are noise. This is the reason healthz is measured at all, and the reason the tolerances are as wide as 16.3% down on throughput. Replacing the driver would raise every number and change no conclusion that has been drawn from them so far. 3. Two locale lookups per page (open, unmeasured) PublicArticleEndpoints.Page calls Locales.Get(http.GetLocale()) twice — once for the render and once for the title. It is a dictionary lookup and almost certainly noise at this scale, but it is the only known redundant work on the request path, so it is written down rather than assumed away. 4. Response compression — 63% of articles throughput (fixed, by moving it) AddNativeWebResponseCompression registered Brotli and gzip for text/html, and UseResponseCompression ran on every page. It was the single most expensive step in the pipeline by a wide margin. Four variants of the same page, same harness, same session: variant articles req/s p50 healthz req/s pipeline with UseResponseCompression() commented out 17,613.3 0.88ms 19,998.8 gzip only, CompressionLevel.Fastest 9,170.4 1.57ms 19,926.1 Brotli + gzip, CompressionLevel.Fastest 6,555.4 2.27ms 19,892.2 Brotli + gzip, provider defaults — as first shipped 6,586.2 2.26ms 19,853.4 Three readings, in order of how much they change the options: The compression level is not the cost. Fastest and the default are 6,555.4 against 6,586.2 — inside the noise. The page is 3,271 bytes; what is being paid for is the per-response codec setup, not the entropy coding, which is why turning the quality knob down buys nothing. The codec is. Dropping Brotli and keeping gzip at Fastest recovers 40% of what was lost. Bottleneck 1 comes back through the middleware. Compressing strips the Content-Length TemplateResults was fixed to declare, so every compressed page is chunked again. Nothing can declare a length ahead of a stream-compressed body, so this half is not tunable — only avoidable. healthz is flat across all four rows, which is what confirms the attribution: the mime list is text/html and a plaintext route never enters the middleware. The A/B that settled it Compression has to happen somewhere, so the question was never whether to pay but where. Measured through a real Caddy 2.11.4 in front of the published binary, same driver, same three-runs-of-10s at concurrency 16: variant articles req/s p50 healthz req/s A — app compresses, direct to Kestrel 7,468.0 2.01ms 20,804.9 B — nothing compresses, direct to Kestrel 18,216.1 0.88ms 21,118.8 C — app compresses, through Caddy 6,254.6 2.50ms 15,777.7 D — Caddy compresses (encode zstd gzip), app does not 9,002.1 1.68ms 16,470.1 E — nothing compresses, through Caddy 14,253.4 1.06ms 15,973.9 C and D are the two real deployment shapes, and D beats C by 44%. The rest is what makes that number readable: the proxy hop alone costs 22% (B → E), compressing at the edge costs 37% of what is left (E → D), and compressing in the app costs 59% (B → A) for a worse result. The edge also gets zstd, which .NET has no provider for: 3,271 bytes of HTML leave Caddy as 1,324. Two structural reasons on top of the number, both found in review rather than in the numbers: UseResponseCompression sat above UseOutputCache in the pipeline order, so every output-cache hit was recompressed. The cache never recovered the codec cost for exactly the public traffic it exists to serve, and moving compression below the cache in-app would have forced Accept-Encoding into the cache key. Buffering in a middleware to restore Content-Length is not an option here: TemplateWriter streams UTF-8 to the body writer by design, and these pages only behave as materialized because 3 KB fits in Kestrel's pipe before a flush. That is an emergent property, not a contract. What changed. UseNativeWebPipeline no longer compresses; scripts/deploy/caddy/Caddyfile and Caddyfile.edge carry encode zstd gzip, and both validate against Caddy 2.11.4. AddNativeWebResponseCompression stays public and registered, so a host with no compressing proxy adds one line and gets the old behaviour back — asserted by CompressionPlacementTests, because losing the encode line would ship every page uncompressed with nothing failing. Brotli is absent from the directive rather than declined: stock Caddy has no encoder for it, and adding br fails config validation with module not registered: http.encoders.br. What the numbers still do not show. The harness measures on loopback with no bandwidth cost, so it flatters every variant that skips compression; on a real WAN client the transfer of 3,271 bytes against 1,324 dominates a 1.4 ms p50 difference. And 16 workers on 16 cores is CPU saturation, which a single-host product will not reach — the throughput deltas are an upper bound on what this matters in production, except for the cache-hit recompression, which scaled with exactly the traffic the cache exists for. What has never been measured Under load with PostgreSQL. Every row here runs against the in-memory store, so the measurement is the render path and the pipeline, not the query path. A JauntyQ-backed run is a different harness and a different baseline. On the deployment target. Every row here was taken on this workstation. The deployment reference carries measured numbers, but what it measured is deploy, restore and rebuild elapsed time. No render or throughput figure has ever been taken on a deployed host. Concurrency other than 16. The harness takes --concurrency; nothing has used it."},{"u":"02-reference-security-baseline.html","t":"Security baseline","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"v2--authentication","t":"V2 — Authentication"},{"i":"v3--session-management","t":"V3 — Session management"},{"i":"v5--input-handling","t":"V5 — Input handling"},{"i":"v13v14--http-security-configuration","t":"V13/V14 — HTTP security configuration"},{"i":"v7v16--logging-and-auditing","t":"V7/V16 — Logging and auditing"},{"i":"v10v1--dependencies-and-build","t":"V10/V1 — Dependencies and build"},{"i":"known-open-items","t":"Known-open items"}],"b":"Security baseline The security posture an app built on NativeWeb inherits, laid out as an ASVS-derived checklist. Each row names where the control lives so a reviewer can verify it rather than trust this document. Rows marked ☐ are known open, with the reason. Applications add their own controls on top. skeleton/docs/security-baseline.md is the copy a stamped product starts from. Account storage, passphrase policy, two-factor and an OAuth authorization server are not part of NativeWeb: libs/nativeweb.signin is a relying party only (decision record), so those controls belong to whichever identity provider an app signs in against. V2 — Authentication ✓ Control Where ☑ Passphrases hashed with Argon2id, fixed cost parameters, NeedsRehash when the cost rises libs/nativeweb.security/Passphrases.cs ☑ Opaque bearer tokens: 32 CSPRNG bytes, only the SHA-256 is stored libs/nativeweb.security/OpaqueTokens.cs ☑ Sign-in by OAuth authorization code with PKCE (S256) libs/nativeweb.signin/OAuthClient.cs ☑ Bot defense: 4 honeypots and a signed time canary on every human form, per-endpoint fake successes libs/nativeweb.security/BotTraps.cs V3 — Session management ✓ Control Where ☑ Opaque server-side sessions (no stateful token on the client), single-delete revocation libs/nativeweb.signin/ISessionStore.cs ☑ __Host- prefixed cookie: Secure, Path=/, no Domain, HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax. Not configurable; a cookie name without the prefix fails at startup libs/nativeweb.signin/SessionManager.cs, SignInOptions.cs ☑ Sign-out deletes the server row and expires the cookie with matching attributes libs/nativeweb.signin/SignInEndpoints.cs ☑ Fixed session lifetime, 14 days by default SignInOptions.SessionLifetime ☑ Antiforgery token on every state-changing form post, a failed token answers 400 before the endpoint runs libs/nativeweb.web/AntiforgeryValidationMiddleware.cs, libs/nativeweb.forms V5 — Input handling ✓ Control Where ☑ Request-body ceiling of 128 KB at Kestrel (MaxRequestBodyBytes); endpoints raise it explicitly where needed libs/nativeweb.web/NativeWebPipeline.cs, NativeWebOptions.cs ☑ Request timeout, 30 s by default NativeWebPipeline.AddNativeWebRequestTimeouts ☑ Rate limiting: per-IP policies general (120 permits) and csp-report (10). Endpoints opt in with RequireRateLimiting NativeWebPipeline.cs, NativeWebOptions.cs ☑ HTML output encoded by default; raw output only through the grep-able Html.Raw libs/nativeweb.render/Html.cs ☑ No reflection-based JSON deserialization of attacker input (AOT source-generated contexts only); /csp-report reads raw bytes repo-wide V13/V14 — HTTP security configuration ✓ Control Where ☑ CSP: default-src 'self', per-request nonce for scripts and styles, frame-ancestors 'none', base-uri 'none', form-action 'self' libs/nativeweb.web/SecurityHeadersMiddleware.cs ☑ Static and error pages get a stricter nonce-free CSP: default-src 'none', no script-src, form-action 'none' SecurityHeadersMiddleware.StaticPageCsp, set by StaticPagesMiddleware and ErrorPageMiddleware ☑ CSP violation reporting: report-uri and Reporting-Endpoints to a rate-limited POST /csp-report, logged raw, never deserialized libs/nativeweb.web/CspReportEndpoint.cs ☑ HSTS for one year with includeSubDomains, outside Development only SecurityHeadersMiddleware.cs ☑ Permissions-Policy denying unused powerful features SecurityHeadersMiddleware.cs ☑ X-Content-Type-Options, X-Frame-Options, Referrer-Policy SecurityHeadersMiddleware.cs ☑ no-store and Pragma: no-cache on sensitive content types and secret-bearing redirects, including HX-Redirect SecurityHeadersMiddleware.cs ☑ security.txt (RFC 9116) at /.well-known/, served only when {Section}:SecurityContact names a mailbox. Unset serves no file, by design libs/nativeweb.web/SecurityTxtEndpoint.cs ☐ Expires in security.txt is a source literal and needs a yearly bump; currently 2027-08-01 SecurityTxtEndpoint.cs V7/V16 — Logging and auditing ✓ Control Where ☑ Structured trap events on every honeypot trip BotTraps.LogTripped ☑ Request logging without bodies or credentials; query strings are redacted libs/nativeweb.web/RequestLoggingMiddleware.cs, QueryRedactingProcessor.cs V10/V1 — Dependencies and build ✓ Control Where ☑ CI CVE gate: dotnet list package --vulnerable --include-transitive fails the build on any finding, or when the audit confirms nothing .github/workflows/ci.yml ☑ TreatWarningsAsErrors repo-wide, including trim and AOT analyzers Directory.Build.props ☑ Central package versions; htmx and Alpine.js vendored, no CDN Directory.Packages.props, apps/sample/wwwroot/vendor ☑ No page HTML cached in localStorage. htmx 2 kept a snapshot cache there, which put rendered authenticated markup in origin-scoped storage that survives sign-out. htmx 4 removed it, so back-navigation re-fetches. The hx-history-cache extension restores the old behaviour and is deliberately not vendored apps/sample/wwwroot/vendor/htmx ☑ Guard responses are not swapped into the page. htmx 4 swaps 4xx/5xx bodies into the target by default; noSwap: [204, 304, \"4xx\", \"5xx\"] keeps a 415 or 400 guard body from rendering inside an authenticated page htmx-config meta in templates/layout/page.html Known-open items security.txt contact address. A deployment that wants a security.txt must set {Section}:SecurityContact, and nothing warns when it has not. Deliberately not a startup guard: refusing to boot without a security contact would be a larger claim than this baseline makes. Canary replay breadth. The bot-trap canary is app-bound, not session, path or single-use. This is an accepted bypass, documented on BotTraps.IssueCanary. Collector address. When Telemetry:Enabled is true and OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT is unset, the exporter targets localhost:4317 and fails in the background. deploy.sh refuses to promote in that state; see deployment.md."},{"u":"02-reference-template-diagnostics.html","t":"NWT template diagnostics","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"at-a-glance","t":"At a glance"},{"i":"the-header-nwt001-nwt002-nwt003","t":"The header: NWT001, NWT002, NWT003"},{"i":"layouts-slots-and-fills-nwt004-nwt007-nwt010-nwt011","t":"Layouts, slots and fills: NWT004, NWT007, NWT010, NWT011"},{"i":"partials-nwt005-nwt006","t":"Partials: NWT005, NWT006"},{"i":"blocks-and-expressions-nwt008-nwt012","t":"Blocks and expressions: NWT008, NWT012"},{"i":"localization-nwt013-nwt014-nwt015","t":"Localization: NWT013, NWT014, NWT015"},{"i":"nwt016--interpolation-outside-a-quoted-attribute-value","t":"NWT016 — interpolation outside a quoted attribute value"}],"b":"NWT template diagnostics Every mistake in a .html template is a build error, never a runtime blank. The nativeweb.render.generator source generator raises them as NWT001–NWT016, category NativeWeb.Render, all DiagnosticSeverity.Error and all enabled by default. There is no suppression story on purpose: a suppressed template error is a page that renders wrong in production instead of failing on the build machine. Each one carries a real file and line, so a build-log entry is clickable. Descriptors: libs/nativeweb.render.generator/TemplateDiagnostics.cs. Every example below is taken from a passing fact in tests/nativeweb.render.tests.unit/GeneratorDiagnosticsTests.cs — if an example here stops triggering its diagnostic, that test file is what to trust. At a glance ID Title Raised when NWT001 Malformed template header The <!--#template … --> header is unclosed, names a reserved @param, or a layout declares @layout NWT002 Unknown template directive A header line is neither @layout nor @param NWT003 Malformed @param Not @param <type> <name> NWT004 Layout not found @layout names no template file NWT005 Partial not found {{> path}} names no template file NWT006 Partial arguments do not match its @param list Missing, unknown or malformed argument; or a layout called as a partial NWT007 Slot or fill misused Six distinct fill/slot mistakes — see below NWT008 Unbalanced template block A block never closed, a closer with no opener, or a reserved loop variable NWT009 Duplicate template class Two template paths compile to one class name NWT010 Parameter conflicts with layout Same @param name, different type, in page and layout NWT011 Required slot not filled Layout declares {{slot X}}, page has no {{fill X}} NWT012 Template expression rejected An expression contains ;, { or } NWT013 Unknown locale key {{t:key}} is not in locales/en.json, or is not a valid key shape NWT014 Orphan locale override A non-English catalog defines a key en.json does not NWT015 Malformed locale catalog Five distinct catalog problems — see below NWT016 Interpolation outside a quoted attribute value {{…}} sits inside a tag but not inside a quoted value The header: NWT001, NWT002, NWT003 The optional header is an HTML comment at the top of the file: <!--#template @layout layout/page @param string Title --> NWT001 covers three things, because all three make the header itself unusable: The comment is never closed with -->. @param uses a reserved name — writer, locale, or any C# keyword. writer is the emitted method's own parameter and locale is the implicit one, so either would shadow machinery the generated code needs. A file that declares {{slot …}} (making it a layout) also declares @layout. Layout chaining is not supported, and a layout gets no class of its own, so without this check the outer layout would silently vanish. Checked before emission, so every page using that layout sees it as broken rather than half-rendered. <!--#template @param string writer --><p>x<\/p> <!-- NWT001: 'writer' is reserved --> NWT002 is the catch-all for a fourth directive: only @layout and @param exist. <!--#template @bogus thing --><p>x<\/p> <!-- NWT002 --> NWT003 is shape alone — @param takes exactly a type and a name. Types are validated here too, so @param My Type Name is NWT003 rather than a C# syntax error inside generated code you did not write. <!--#template @param OnlyOneToken --><p>x<\/p> <!-- NWT003 --> Layouts, slots and fills: NWT004, NWT007, NWT010, NWT011 NWT004 — @layout layout/absent matches no template file. Paths are the template path without extension, so templates/layout/page.html is layout/page. NWT007 is one ID over six conditions, all of them \"the fill/slot contract is broken\": Condition Example {{fill X}} has no matching slot in the layout layout has {{slot Main}}, page fills Ghost {{fill …}} with no @layout directive at all a page that fills nothing in particular {{fill X}} appears more than once two {{fill Main}} blocks {{fill …}} is not at the top level a fill nested inside {{if}} Both {{fill Main}} and top-level content one or the other, not both The layout declares no {{slot Main}} page content would be silently dropped The last one is the reason this is an error rather than a warning: a layout with only optional slots swallows the page body with nothing to show for it. NWT010 — the page and its layout both declare a @param of the same name with different types. The composed template has one parameter list, so the two cannot both be right. NWT011 — the layout declares {{slot Sidebar}} and the page never fills it. Mark the slot optional with {{slot? Sidebar}} if that is intended; an unfilled {{slot? …}} is fine. Partials: NWT005, NWT006 NWT005 — {{> shared/absent}} matches no template file. NWT006 covers every way a call can disagree with the callee's @param list, and reports all of them in one message rather than one per build: Missing a required argument. Passing an argument the partial does not declare. A malformed argument: they are Name=expression with no spaces in the expression, so {{> shared/field Label=\"Hello world\"}} is NWT006. Compute the value in the calling C# and pass it as a parameter. Calling a layout as a partial. A file with slots is a layout; it cannot be invoked with {{> …}}. Blocks and expressions: NWT008, NWT012 NWT008 — {{if}}, {{for}} and {{fill}} all open a block that must close. It fires on an unclosed opener, on a closer with no opener ({{/if}} alone), and on a {{for}} whose loop variable is not a valid identifier or is reserved ({{for locale in Items}}). NWT012 — a template expression may not contain ;, { or }. Expressions are values, not statements; anything with control flow or a statement separator belongs in the calling C#. This is a hard syntactic bar rather than a parse of what the expression does, which is the point: it cannot be argued around. <p>{{X; System.IO.File.Delete(\"x\")}}<\/p> <!-- NWT012 --> Localization: NWT013, NWT014, NWT015 locales/en.json is the root catalog and the fallthrough for every locale. NWT013 — {{t:key}} where the key is absent from en.json, or is not a valid key shape (letters, digits, ., _, -). NWT014 — the inverse: locales/ms.json defines a key en.json does not. Overrides refine the English root, they do not extend it, so this is almost always a typo. NWT015 covers the catalog files themselves: Condition Example en.json is missing entirely no root catalog to fall through to A value is not a string or a nested object { \"app\": [1, 2] } An _-prefixed metadata key has an object value { \"_meta\": { \"x\": \"y\" } } _dir is not ltr or rtl { \"_dir\": \"sideways\" } Two locale ids collide on the generated member name pt-br.json and pt_br.json, or all.json colliding with Locales.All A nested _-prefixed key is metadata, not a string key: {\"app\": {\"_note\": …}} does not become app._note and does not trip NWT014. NWT016 — interpolation outside a quoted attribute value The newest and the only one that exists purely as a security guard. TemplateWriter encodes <, >, &, \" and ' — and nothing else. That set is safe in text and inside a quoted attribute value, and nowhere else. A space is not encoded, so a value interpolated into an unquoted attribute can close it and open onfocus=…; interpolating into the attribute-name position is the same escape by a shorter route. TemplateWriter's own comment had asserted this invariant since day one and nothing enforced it. NWT016 is what makes it checkable. Rejected: <div class={{Value}}>x<\/div> <div class = {{Value}}>x<\/div> <div data-x=prefix{{Value}}>x<\/div> <div {{Value}}>x<\/div> <div class=\"a\" {{Value}}>x<\/div> <div title={{t:app.name}}>x<\/div> Accepted — quote the value, single or double: <div class=\"{{Value}}\">x<\/div> The negative cases carry more weight than the positive ones here. A guard that fires on ordinary markup gets deleted rather than obeyed, so GeneratorDiagnosticsTests pins a longer list of shapes that must not raise NWT016 than of shapes that must."},{"u":"02-reference-versioning.html","t":"Versioning and release","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"one-version-every-package","t":"One version, every package"},{"i":"what-each-component-means","t":"What each component means"},{"i":"what-counts-as-breaking","t":"What counts as breaking"},{"i":"support-window","t":"Support window"},{"i":"retiring-a-library","t":"Retiring a library"},{"i":"where-the-packages-go-and-where-they-do-not","t":"Where the packages go, and where they do not"},{"i":"cutting-a-release","t":"Cutting a release"},{"i":"related","t":"Related"}],"b":"Versioning and release What a NativeWeb.* version number promises, what breaks one, how long a released minor is supported, and how a library is retired. It is the release process for a library, as required by decision 004. One version, every package Every NativeWeb.* package carries the same version, from a single VersionPrefix in Directory.Build.props. There is no per-library version and there will not be one. That is a deliberate loss of precision. Extrode.NativeWeb.Mail changes far less often than Extrode.NativeWeb.Scaffold, so lockstep versioning means it gets minor bumps that changed nothing in it. The alternative costs more: libraries that reference each other, versioned independently, produce a matrix where Extrode.NativeWeb.Web 0.4.0 and Extrode.NativeWeb.Render 0.2.0 restore together, compile, and were never built or tested together once. PackageVersionTests enforces the single version, and tools/pack.ts refuses to run when Directory.Build.props declares more than one. version what it means X.Y.Z a release. Built from a vX.Y.Z tag by .github/workflows/publish.yml, and the only thing ever pushed to the feed X.Y.Z-dev every other build. What a local deno run -A tools/pack.ts produces, and what CI packs on every push The suffix is opt-in in the safe direction: a release requires IsReleaseBuild=true, which only the publish workflow passes. An unmarked pack cannot produce something that looks like a release. What each component means Pre-1.0, and this framework is 0.x, semver's own rule is that anything may change in a minor. That is not a useful promise to a consumer, so the stricter reading below applies now and becomes the literal semver reading at 1.0. bump means major a seam changed shape. Consumer code stops compiling, or compiles and behaves differently. Pre-1.0 this is the minor position: 0.1.x → 0.2.0 minor new capability, nothing removed and nothing re-shaped. Pre-1.0 this shares the minor position, so a 0.x minor bump may or may not break; the entry in breaking-changes.md is what says which patch a defect fixed or a behaviour corrected with no signature change. 0.1.0 → 0.1.1 The reliable answer is not the number, it is breaking-changes.md. Read it top-down from the version you are on and stop at the entry you have already taken. Every entry names the seam, what changed and the before/after a consumer edits by. What counts as breaking A change is breaking when a consumer who upgrades and rebuilds gets a compile error, or gets a build that succeeds and does something else. Concretely, in this framework: A public seam's signature changes. IScaffoldStore<T>.ListAsync gaining an ordering and a cursor (see breaking-changes.md) is the worked example — every hand-written store had to be edited, and nothing generated did. A generator's diagnostic set gains a member that fires on code that used to build. NWF006 is not breaking (nothing could declare a file field before it existed); a new NWF00n that rejects a model shape that used to compile is. A configuration key changes name, default, or failure mode. Everything in configuration.md is a seam, and the On a bad value column is the part of it a deployment depends on. Turning a silent fallback into a startup failure is breaking even though no code changed. A library is removed, which is the case with the longest reach and is covered below. Not breaking: an added optional parameter with a default; a new type; a widened accepted input; a performance change; anything under apps/sample, skeleton/ or tests/, none of which a consumer references. A breaking change is not a defect, and this repository would rather break a seam than carry a shim that makes the wrong shape survivable. What is owed instead is the breaking-changes.md entry. Support window The current minor, and the one before it. When 0.3.0 releases, 0.2.x still receives security fixes and defect fixes that apply cleanly; 0.1.x receives nothing. That is short on purpose. The cost of an upgrade is a rebuild and a read of breaking-changes.md. A longer window would commit this repository to backporting into a shape it has already moved past. If consumers need more time than that, this section is the first thing that has to change. A fix to a supported older minor releases as a patch on that minor: 0.2.4 alongside 0.3.1. VersionPrefix is bumped on a branch cut from that minor's tag, and the same tag-triggered publish runs. Retiring a library The path a departing library takes: Deprecate in place, in a minor. The library still ships and still works. Its README.md gains a line at the top naming what replaces it and the version it is expected to go in, and an entry lands in breaking-changes.md under the deprecation date. Ship at least one release in that state, so a consumer that upgrades sees the notice before the library disappears rather than at the same moment. Remove in the next breaking bump, with a second breaking-changes.md entry naming the removal and the migration. A consumer pinned to 0.2.x is not moved by a removal in 0.3.0 until it chooses to be. Decision 007 records a removal that skipped these steps. Where the packages go, and where they do not .github/workflows/publish.yml pushes to nuget.org (https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json) with the NUGET_API_KEY repository secret. The package ids are Extrode.NativeWeb.*, matching JauntyQ's Extrode.JauntyQ.*; namespaces and assembly names stay NativeWeb.*. No version has been published yet. No packable library carries JauntyQ-generated output. grep -rn -i jauntyq libs/ --include=*.csproj finds comments only; the two projects that reference JauntyQ are apps/sample and the stamped product under skeleton/, neither of which is packable. tools/pack.ts refuses a package containing a JauntyQ path, which keeps that claim true: a library that grows a JauntyQ reference would otherwise reach the feed, and the JauntyQ licence bars that (see AGENTS.md). Cutting a release Read the branch's CHANGELOG.md under ## [Unreleased] and decide the number against What each component means above. Bump VersionPrefix in Directory.Build.props and the five Extrode.NativeWeb.* pins in skeleton/Directory.Packages.props — PackageVersionTests fails if they disagree. Move the changelog's ## [Unreleased] entries under a ## [X.Y.Z] heading, dated. Merge dev into main with --no-ff, tag vX.Y.Z, and push the tag. The tag is what publishes. publish.yml refuses a tag that disagrees with VersionPrefix before it packs anything, so a mistyped tag costs a failed run rather than a burned version — and a version on nuget.org can never be deleted or replaced, only unlisted. Related breaking-changes.md — what changed at each seam, and the edit. configuration.md — the settings this document treats as seams. ../decisions/2026-08-30-004-package-mode-for-consumers.md — why consumers take packages rather than a checkout. tools/pack.ts — the pack command, and what it verifies in each produced .nupkg."}];
